{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Wg1dN5yLi82VYvcj7RWscn","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# CAMPAIGN campaign-4 — constitution (opus-5)\n2\t\n3\tYou are a research engineer working this problem on your own. No one is available to answer\n4\tquestions while the run is in progress, so make the call yourself and record it in your journal.\n5\tKeep working until the landing order arrives. What you are working on:\n6\t\n7\t> A calibration problem: build the price-simulation model with the lowest CRPS you can reach. CRPS measures how well your predicted distribution matches what actually happened, and lower is better. Your model must handle all three scoring configurations (CRYPTO_24H, COM_EQU_24H, CRYPTO_1H) and is measured over the 10 days following your data cutoff. Judge every experiment on CRPS; the constitution explains why the field-relative position is a poor target. Choose your own research questions. Find something publishable — honest failures count too.\n8\t\n9\t## Context: Synth (Bittensor SN50)\n10\t\n11\tSynth is a Bittensor subnet where \"miners\" serve probabilistic price simulations: for a given\n12\tasset and start time, a miner returns N simulated price paths (a \"fan\"). A validator then\n13\tcompares these paths against the actually observed prices and scores each miner on **CRPS**\n14\t(Continuous Ranked Probability Score — lower = better), summed across several horizons. A good\n15\tminer produces well-calibrated distributions: neither too narrow (overconfidence is punished)\n16\tnor too wide (imprecision is punished).\n17\t\n18\t## The three scoring configurations\n19\t\n20\tThe subnet scores three configurations across two horizons (its code calls them *competitions*;\n21\t`synth.validator.competition_config` is the source of truth). Your model has to handle all three —\n22\ta `simulate()` that ignores `time_increment`/`time_length` and assumes a 24h horizon breaks on\n23\tcrypto-1h.\n24\t\n25\t| Competition | Assets | Horizon | Step | Points/path |\n26\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n27\t| CRYPTO_24H | BTC, ETH, SOL, XRP, HYPE | 24h (86400 s) | 5min (300 s) | 289 |\n28\t| COM_EQU_24H | XAU, NVDAX, GOOGLX, TSLAX, AAPLX, WTIOIL, SPCX, SP500 (+ SPYX, retired — see the breaks table) | 24h | 5min | 289 |\n29\t| CRYPTO_1H | BTC, ETH, SOL, XRP, HYPE | 1h (3600 s) | 1min (60 s) | 61 |\n30\t\n31\t**1000 simulations per prompt** in all three cases (`PromptConfig.num_simulations`); a response\n32\twhose path count differs is rejected outright by the validator\n33\t(`synth.validator.response_validation_v2`). `simulate()` receives `num_simulations` as an\n34\targument — honour whatever it is given, never hardcode a path count. Lower it only to speed up\n35\tyour own iteration loops, never in what your champion serves.\n36\t\n37\tScoring differs substantially between the two horizons.\n38\tThe exact definition (source of truth): `synth.validator.competition_config` and\n39\t`synth.validator.crps_calculation` in the synth-subnet repo. Read them.\n40\t\n41\t## What you are optimising\n42\t\n43\t**CRPS is the objective. Lower is better.** It is the only quantity that measures your model\n44\tdirectly: it compares your predicted distribution against what actually happened, prompt by\n45\tprompt, and it does not depend on what anyone else submitted.\n46\t\n47\tA field-relative position is also reported, and it is tempting to optimise because it looks like a\n48\tresult. **Do not.** The submissions are tightly packed, and the per-prompt score the aggregation\n49\tconsumes is your CRPS *minus the lowest CRPS on that prompt* — which re-bases a small difference\n50\tonto a much smaller number. A tenth of a percent of CRPS can therefore move a position by a\n51\thundred places in either direction. That makes position a loud, unstable readout of a tiny change:\n52\tuseful for checking you are in the right regime, useless as a target. Two runs of the same model\n53\tcan land far apart on it without differing in any way that matters.\n54\t\n55\tSo: **judge every experiment on CRPS**, per configuration and per asset. Report position if you\n56\tlike, but never trade a CRPS regression for a better one.\n57\t\n58\tHow the reported position is formed, for completeness: the aggregation does not average across\n59\tassets. It weights each asset by `ASSET_COEFFICIENTS` (`synth.validator.moving_average`),\n60\tnormalizes per miner, then applies a softmax, so a high-coefficient asset carries much more\n61\tweight. Your champion is measured over the **10 days following your data cutoff**, with each of\n62\tthe three configurations contributing one third regardless of asset count. The moving average\n63\tbackfills a model that appears late with the worst value in the set, so generation starts one\n64\thorizon before the window and yours is never treated as late.\n65\t\n66\t## Your resources\n67\t\n68\t- **Your workspace**: a standalone git repo. `agent/` contains a minimal starter model\n69\t  (`modeling.py`, log-normal martingale — REPLACE IT), an evaluator (`predict.py`) that you can improve, and\n70\t  `pyproject.toml` with pinned dependencies. First action: `uv sync`. Then run EVERY python\n71\t  command through `uv run` (`uv run python -c ...`, `uv run agent/predict.py`): the container's\n72\t  system `python3` has no numpy or pandas — only the workspace venv does.\n73\t  `agent/predict.py` is also used for the 1h format (pass `--time-length 3600 --time-increment\n74\t  60`).\n75\t- **`market_data/`**: a frozen snapshot of minute OHLCV (parquet partitions\n76\t  `prices/{ASSET}/1m/date=YYYY-MM-DD.parquet`, columns `open, high, low, close, volume,\n77\t  trade_count`, resolve it with\n78\t  `synth_lib.preparation.config.default_store_root`, do not hardcode either) covering\n79\t  **2025-08-01 → 2026-09-14**, mounted\n80\t  READ-ONLY, at the ROOT of the workspace. This is your ONLY source of market data. **There is no\n81\t  download step and no `prepare.py`**: the data is already in place, complete over that window,\n82\t  and the mount is read-only. Fetching prices is forbidden (see hard rules).\n83\t\n84\t  Each series is the venue the asset is actually SCORED against — Binance spot for BTC/ETH/SOL/\n85\t  XRP, Hyperliquid for everything else — one instrument per asset over its whole history. The\n86\t  history reaches back to each market's genuine FIRST TRADE, so depth varies by asset:\n87\t\n88\t  | From | Assets |\n89\t  |---|---|\n90\t  | 2025-08-01 | BTC, ETH, SOL, XRP, HYPE |\n91\t  | 2025-11-12 → 11-21 | NVDAX, TSLAX, GOOGLX, AAPLX |\n92\t  | 2025-12-22 / 2026-01-06 | XAU / WTIOIL |\n93\t  | 2026-03-18 / 2026-05-17 | SP500 / SPCX |\n94\t\n95\t  **Read `DATA.md` at the workspace root FIRST**: it is generated from this exact snapshot at\n96\t  setup and carries the measured truth — the partition schema, NaN semantics (NaN close = no\n97\t  trade that minute, NOT market hours; missing file = before the market's first trade), and a\n98\t  per-asset coverage table with density by era. Two caveats it quantifies that will bite if\n99\t  ignored: the early months of the newer Hyperliquid listings are genuinely THIN (low realized\n100\t  volatility there is real, not smoothing), and these are venue-specific perpetual prices with\n101\t  their own basis (xyz:SP500 trades around 7,500 — not the cash index).\n102\t\n103\t  **The venue never closes; the underlying does.** Minutes exist around the clock for every asset,\n104\t  so there is no session gap in the DATA — but the equity and commodity perps track markets that\n105\t  shut, and their VOLATILITY follows those hours closely. Measured on the snapshot: realized\n106\t  volatility per minute swings about 4-5x across the UTC day for the tokenised equities and gold,\n107\t  peaking at the US cash open and bottoming overnight, and weekdays run 2-4x hotter than weekends.\n108\t  The crypto assets, genuinely 24/7, are far flatter on both. A constant per-asset volatility is\n109\t  therefore too wide overnight and too narrow at the open, and CRPS charges for both. Measure the\n110\t  seasonality yourself before you model it, and check whether it holds for each asset rather than\n111\t  assuming one shape fits the group.\n112\t\n113\t  Do not \"repair\" NaN gaps by interpolating; scoring already drops NaN per prompt, and inventing\n114\t  prices in the gaps is how you fool your own validation.\n115\t\n116\t  **Fitting vs comparing**: the FULL price history is yours to fit, train and calibrate on. The\n117\t  2026-06-23 configuration split (breaks table below) constrains only FIELD-RELATIVE numbers —\n118\t  never compute one on a window that starts before it. CRPS itself is unaffected.\n119\t\n120\t  **Three structural breaks in the subnet's history. Do not backtest across them:**\n121\t\n122\t  | Date | What changed |\n123\t  |---|---|\n124\t  | 2026-06-23 | The subnet split into the 3 current competitions. Before it: 2 profiles, and COM_EQU_24H did not exist. A field-relative rank spanning this date mixes incompatible reward structures — `synth_lib` will warn, and the number it gives you is meaningless. |\n125\t  | 2026-03-11 | The crypto-1h CRPS formula changed. Ranks before it are not comparable to today's. |\n126\t  | ~2026-07-17 | SPYX was retired and SP500 introduced as a NEW asset, carrying the HIGHEST coefficient (3.44) in COM_EQU_24H. **SPYX is DEAD**: its feed stopped, the validator no longer prompts or scores it, and no post-mid-July prices exist — it still appears in the pinned competition config, but spend NOTHING on it. SP500 has scored field history from ~2026-07-19 onward; fit on its whole price series, validate its RANK on the scored weeks. |\n127\t\n128\t  **Loader expectations**: synth-lib's strict `load_range` RAISES on any NaN or missing day in\n129\t  the requested range — that is a feature for scoring, a trap for exploration. `agent/predict.py`\n130\t  shows the sanctioned lenient pattern; prior agents each rediscovered this at their own expense.\n131\t\n132\t  **Always run your commands from the workspace root**: synth-lib's backtester resolves the\n133\t  price store relative to the current directory (`MinutePriceStore(asset)` with no root, see\n134\t  `_price_store`). From a subfolder it would find nothing and would try to download the missing\n135\t  days — which the read-only mount refuses. `predict.py` already forces this directory and\n136\t  checks coverage before scoring; if you write your own evaluation script, reproduce these two\n137\t  safeguards.\n138\t- **synth-lib** (installed; public source: github.com/synthdataco/synth-lib): the official\n139\t  backtester — `from synth_lib.backtester.orchestration import backtest` — which computes CRPS with\n140\t  the validator's exact CRPS function, and can also place you among the recorded field. Use it\n141\t  via `agent/predict.py`; NEVER reimplement the metric. The backtester runs FULLY OFFLINE here:\n142\t  `SYNTH_BACKTESTER_OFFLINE_DATA_ROOT` is set in your environment and points at a bundle of\n143\t  historical scores inside your snapshot, so no API call is needed. DATA.md gives the measured\n144\t  span of scored prompts it holds: a field-relative number outside that span has nothing to\n145\t  compare against, though CRPS is still computable. DATA.md also gives the span of the cached\n146\t  realized paths, which are a different thing — they stand in for minutes your price store is\n147\t  missing.\n148\t- **synth-subnet** (a synth-lib dependency; source: github.com/synthdataco/synth-subnet): the\n149\t  validator's code. Read it to understand exactly how you'll be scored.\n150\t- **Public Synth API** (api.synthdata.co): historical field scores\n151\t  (`/validation/scores/historical`, query windows ≤ 2 days) and rewards (`/rewards/scores`).\n152\t  synth-lib handles pagination for you. Studying the field (CRPS levels, what the top miners do)\n153\t  is ALLOWED and recommended; copying the paths served by an individual miner is\n154\t  FORBIDDEN.\n155\t- **Miner dashboard analytics API** (`https://monitoring.synthdata.co/v1`, schema at\n156\t  `https://monitoring.synthdata.co/v1/openapi.json`): the public backend behind the miner\n157\t  dashboard — scoreboards, leaderboards, reward pools. Read the OpenAPI schema to discover the\n158\t  endpoints. Same rule as the scores API: studying the field is encouraged, copying an\n159\t  individual miner's served paths is not.\n160\t- **Pretrained models & Hugging Face**: allowed and in scope. The sandbox has network access to\n161\t  the HF Hub and PyPI; the GPU is yours and sits idle unless you use it. Time-series foundation\n162\t  models, fine-tuning, or classical ML are all legitimate strategies — as is deciding, with\n163\t  evidence, that they don't improve on closed-form calibration. If you go this way: download weights\n164\t  EARLY (bandwidth is part of your clock), set `HF_HOME` inside your workspace, and remember\n165\t  your champion must run OFFLINE — weights must be committed to your git workspace, total under\n166\t  a few GB (the harness collects your repo as a git bundle).\n167\t- **Serving latency**: the live validator gives you roughly the prompt's lead time (~1 minute)\n168\t  to respond — that is the hard bound. But the evaluation of your champion generates several\n169\t  thousand prompts, and your own backtests will too: at more than a few seconds per prompt\n170\t  (1000 paths, this hardware), your iteration loop and your evaluation both become the\n171\t  bottleneck. Measure your per-prompt latency early and treat ~5 s as the practical ceiling.\n172\t- **The web**: open for knowledge — searching the literature (arXiv, papers, reference\n173\t  implementations) is encouraged — but NEVER for fetching prices or market data (see hard\n174\t  rules).\n175\t\n176\t## Your envelope\n177\t\n178\t- Credit budget: 100.0 USD. Deadline: 18 h of wall-clock time.\n179\t- The `BUDGET.md` file in your workspace is maintained by the harness: READ IT AT THE START OF\n180\t  EACH ITERATION. When it contains a LANDING ORDER, it is non-negotiable.\n181\t- Hardware: GCP g2-standard-16: 16 vCPU / 64GB RAM / 1x NVIDIA L4 (24GB VRAM). Your sandbox gets 14 CPU, 48GB RAM and the GPU. torch is already in your pyproject — no need to add it.. ONLY ONE GPU job at a time; cap the memory of your heavy jobs.\n182\t- **You are expected to use your envelope.** Iterating is the job: measure, change one thing,\n183\t  measure again. **Nominating is final** — the first time you stop while `agent/CHAMPION`\n184\t  exists, your campaign is over: you will not be resumed, and every remaining dollar and hour\n185\t  is forfeited. Until then you may keep iterating and amend your nomination. Nominate early\n186\t  only when your journal contains EVIDENCE that further iteration would not improve your Score.\n187\t  **A champion is MANDATORY.** What is scored is how strong your champion is, not whether you dared\n188\t  to nominate one: ending with no `agent/CHAMPION` is a Score of 0 and a wasted leg — strictly worse\n189\t  than nominating something imperfect.\n190\t\n191\t## Constraints (they are what make the result valid)\n192\t\n193\t1. **No-lookahead**: a prediction at `start_time` uses only information available at\n194\t   `start_time`. Your champion is evaluated on the 10 days FOLLOWING your data cutoff: treat\n195\t   everything after **2026-09-14** as nonexistent. Querying ANY API (scores, rewards,\n196\t   dashboard) for times after 2026-09-14 is lookahead into your own evaluation window and\n197\t   invalidates the result. Audited on your transcript.\n198\t2. **Prices = snapshot only.** No fetching market data online (price APIs, exchanges,\n199\t   aggregators). The web is for knowledge only. Audited on your transcript.\n200\t3. **Scoring = synth-lib.** Every CRPS evaluation goes through `synth_lib.backtester`\n201\t   (via `agent/predict.py`). One implementation of the metric is what makes results comparable.\n202\t4. Not allowed: falsifying the journal or results; reproducing another participant's served\n203\t   output as your own.\n204\t5. **Keep binary and encoded data out of your terminal output.** Read parquet with pandas and\n205\t   `.npy` with numpy — never `cat` a parquet, a git bundle, or model weights, and never pipe\n206\t   base64 into your context. A screenful of encoded bytes buys you nothing, costs context, and\n207\t   is a known trigger for content classifiers that will end your turn.\n208\t6. **Start from public sources only**: synth-lib, synth-subnet, the Synth API, and your own\n209\t   ideas. The operator's existing miner models are not part of your materials and must not\n210\t   inform your work. Nothing of that kind belongs in your environment; if any of it turns up\n211\t   there, leave it unread and note it in `suggestions.md`.\n212\t7. Your final champion must expose, in `agent/modeling.py`:\n213\t   `simulate(asset, start_time, time_increment, time_length, num_simulations, context_prices)`\n214\t   -> tuple `(start_timestamp, time_increment, *paths)` — each path: a list of\n215\t   `time_length//time_increment + 1` floats starting at the last price in the context.\n216\t   **The `context_prices` contract, exactly**: a minute-indexed pd.DataFrame over the 7 days\n217\t   preceding `start_time`, with columns `open, high, low, close, volume, trade_count`. `close`\n218\t   is the series you are scored against; the rest are context. It MAY CONTAIN NaN in every\n219\t   column — your `simulate()` must tolerate them (drop, never interpolate). Evaluation passes\n220\t   the raw frame: handle gaps inside your model, not in a loader you hope evaluation shares.\n221\t   Runnable OFFLINE (weights/artifacts committed or included in the workspace).\n222\t8. **If your model uses randomness at all, seed it from `SYNTH_BENCHMARK_SEED`.** Nothing\n223\t   here asks you to introduce randomness — a deterministic champion ignores this rule. If you\n224\t   do use it, read the variable once at the top of `simulate()` and seed every generator your\n225\t   model touches from it: Python's `random`, NumPy, torch, and anything carrying its own. It\n226\t   is absent during your own runs and set only at evaluation, so fall back to a seed of your\n227\t   choosing when it is missing. What you may never do is hardcode a seed that ignores the\n228\t   variable, or leave a generator unseeded so the clock or OS entropy supplies it.\n229\t\n230\t## Required deliverables (in `agent/`)\n231\t\n232\tWrite `journal.md`, `report.md`, and `suggestions.md` in English — these reports are published\n233\tto the Synth miner community.\n234\t\n235\t- `journal.md`: one entry per iteration — Hypothesis / Change / Result (numbers) / Verdict\n236\t  (keep-discard) / Lesson. As you go: this is your memory if you crash.\n237\t- `report.md`: your final report, explainer style (audience = Synth miners). Sections: context,\n238\t  approach, experiments (failures included), results, limitations, what you'd do with more\n239\t  budget.\n240\t- `CHAMPION`: YAML — `sha` (an EXISTING git commit in your workspace, not a placeholder),\n241\t  `agent_dir: agent`, `profiles`, `notes`. `profiles` must list the formats your champion\n242\t  supports: `[low, high]` if you cover both 24h AND 1h (expected), `[low]` if you could only\n243\t  handle the 24h. `notes` MUST include your champion's backtested **CRPS per configuration**,\n244\t  produced by the official backtester (`agent/predict.py`) at 1000 paths, alongside the CRPS of\n245\t  whatever you started from — a nomination without measured numbers is incomplete. The harness checks the interface as soon as\n246\t  this file appears (result in BUDGET.md) — fix it if the check fails.\n247\t- `suggestions.md`: anything that falls outside your scope.\n248\t- you must commit all deliverables to your workspace git repo before the deadline.\n249\t\n250\t## Method (your choice, but recommended)\n251\t\n252\tYou choose your own research questions, hypotheses, splits, and criteria. What works here:\n253\twrite the hypothesis BEFORE the experiment, pre-registered kill thresholds, validation on 2\n254\twindows of opposite regimes (within the limits of the snapshot), one variable at a time,\n255\tfrequent commits. Watch out for the edges of eval windows: only backtest windows fully covered\n256\tby the snapshot.\n257\t\n258\t## Resuming after a crash\n259\t\n260\tIf you restart with no context: reread this file, then `BUDGET.md`, then your `journal.md`,\n261\tand continue where you left off.\n262\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e0fdc998-0d4c-4bec-aa01-713aae481473","uuid":"de743c59-07e8-4ef5-8230-5a8fed7acfa8","timestamp":"2026-09-29T16:52:10.808Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/workspace/agent/CAMPAIGN.md","content":"# CAMPAIGN campaign-4 — constitution (opus-5)\n\nYou are a research engineer working this problem on your own. No one is available to answer\nquestions while the run is in progress, so make the call yourself and record it in your journal.\nKeep working until the landing order arrives. What you are working on:\n\n> A calibration problem: build the price-simulation model with the lowest CRPS you can reach. CRPS measures how well your predicted distribution matches what actually happened, and lower is better. Your model must handle all three scoring configurations (CRYPTO_24H, COM_EQU_24H, CRYPTO_1H) and is measured over the 10 days following your data cutoff. Judge every experiment on CRPS; the constitution explains why the field-relative position is a poor target. Choose your own research questions. Find something publishable — honest failures count too.\n\n## Context: Synth (Bittensor SN50)\n\nSynth is a Bittensor subnet where \"miners\" serve probabilistic price simulations: for a given\nasset and start time, a miner returns N simulated price paths (a \"fan\"). A validator then\ncompares these paths against the actually observed prices and scores each miner on **CRPS**\n(Continuous Ranked Probability Score — lower = better), summed across several horizons. A good\nminer produces well-calibrated distributions: neither too narrow (overconfidence is punished)\nnor too wide (imprecision is punished).\n\n## The three scoring configurations\n\nThe subnet scores three configurations across two horizons (its code calls them *competitions*;\n`synth.validator.competition_config` is the source of truth). Your model has to handle all three —\na `simulate()` that ignores `time_increment`/`time_length` and assumes a 24h horizon breaks on\ncrypto-1h.\n\n| Competition | Assets | Horizon | Step | Points/path |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| CRYPTO_24H | BTC, ETH, SOL, XRP, HYPE | 24h (86400 s) | 5min (300 s) | 289 |\n| COM_EQU_24H | XAU, NVDAX, GOOGLX, TSLAX, AAPLX, WTIOIL, SPCX, SP500 (+ SPYX, retired — see the breaks table) | 24h | 5min | 289 |\n| CRYPTO_1H | BTC, ETH, SOL, XRP, HYPE | 1h (3600 s) | 1min (60 s) | 61 |\n\n**1000 simulations per prompt** in all three cases (`PromptConfig.num_simulations`); a response\nwhose path count differs is rejected outright by the validator\n(`synth.validator.response_validation_v2`). `simulate()` receives `num_simulations` as an\nargument — honour whatever it is given, never hardcode a path count. Lower it only to speed up\nyour own iteration loops, never in what your champion serves.\n\nScoring differs substantially between the two horizons.\nThe exact definition (source of truth): `synth.validator.competition_config` and\n`synth.validator.crps_calculation` in the synth-subnet repo. Read them.\n\n## What you are optimising\n\n**CRPS is the objective. Lower is better.** It is the only quantity that measures your model\ndirectly: it compares your predicted distribution against what actually happened, prompt by\nprompt, and it does not depend on what anyone else submitted.\n\nA field-relative position is also reported, and it is tempting to optimise because it looks like a\nresult. **Do not.** The submissions are tightly packed, and the per-prompt score the aggregation\nconsumes is your CRPS *minus the lowest CRPS on that prompt* — which re-bases a small difference\nonto a much smaller number. A tenth of a percent of CRPS can therefore move a position by a\nhundred places in either direction. That makes position a loud, unstable readout of a tiny change:\nuseful for checking you are in the right regime, useless as a target. Two runs of the same model\ncan land far apart on it without differing in any way that matters.\n\nSo: **judge every experiment on CRPS**, per configuration and per asset. Report position if you\nlike, but never trade a CRPS regression for a better one.\n\nHow the reported position is formed, for completeness: the aggregation does not average across\nassets. It weights each asset by `ASSET_COEFFICIENTS` (`synth.validator.moving_average`),\nnormalizes per miner, then applies a softmax, so a high-coefficient asset carries much more\nweight. Your champion is measured over the **10 days following your data cutoff**, with each of\nthe three configurations contributing one third regardless of asset count. The moving average\nbackfills a model that appears late with the worst value in the set, so generation starts one\nhorizon before the window and yours is never treated as late.\n\n## Your resources\n\n- **Your workspace**: a standalone git repo. `agent/` contains a minimal starter model\n  (`modeling.py`, log-normal martingale — REPLACE IT), an evaluator (`predict.py`) that you can improve, and\n  `pyproject.toml` with pinned dependencies. First action: `uv sync`. Then run EVERY python\n  command through `uv run` (`uv run python -c ...`, `uv run agent/predict.py`): the container's\n  system `python3` has no numpy or pandas — only the workspace venv does.\n  `agent/predict.py` is also used for the 1h format (pass `--time-length 3600 --time-increment\n  60`).\n- **`market_data/`**: a frozen snapshot of minute OHLCV (parquet partitions\n  `prices/{ASSET}/1m/date=YYYY-MM-DD.parquet`, columns `open, high, low, close, volume,\n  trade_count`, resolve it with\n  `synth_lib.preparation.config.default_store_root`, do not hardcode either) covering\n  **2025-08-01 → 2026-09-14**, mounted\n  READ-ONLY, at the ROOT of the workspace. This is your ONLY source of market data. **There is no\n  download step and no `prepare.py`**: the data is already in place, complete over that window,\n  and the mount is read-only. Fetching prices is forbidden (see hard rules).\n\n  Each series is the venue the asset is actually SCORED against — Binance spot for BTC/ETH/SOL/\n  XRP, Hyperliquid for everything else — one instrument per asset over its whole history. The\n  history reaches back to each market's genuine FIRST TRADE, so depth varies by asset:\n\n  | From | Assets |\n  |---|---|\n  | 2025-08-01 | BTC, ETH, SOL, XRP, HYPE |\n  | 2025-11-12 → 11-21 | NVDAX, TSLAX, GOOGLX, AAPLX |\n  | 2025-12-22 / 2026-01-06 | XAU / WTIOIL |\n  | 2026-03-18 / 2026-05-17 | SP500 / SPCX |\n\n  **Read `DATA.md` at the workspace root FIRST**: it is generated from this exact snapshot at\n  setup and carries the measured truth — the partition schema, NaN semantics (NaN close = no\n  trade that minute, NOT market hours; missing file = before the market's first trade), and a\n  per-asset coverage table with density by era. Two caveats it quantifies that will bite if\n  ignored: the early months of the newer Hyperliquid listings are genuinely THIN (low realized\n  volatility there is real, not smoothing), and these are venue-specific perpetual prices with\n  their own basis (xyz:SP500 trades around 7,500 — not the cash index).\n\n  **The venue never closes; the underlying does.** Minutes exist around the clock for every asset,\n  so there is no session gap in the DATA — but the equity and commodity perps track markets that\n  shut, and their VOLATILITY follows those hours closely. Measured on the snapshot: realized\n  volatility per minute swings about 4-5x across the UTC day for the tokenised equities and gold,\n  peaking at the US cash open and bottoming overnight, and weekdays run 2-4x hotter than weekends.\n  The crypto assets, genuinely 24/7, are far flatter on both. A constant per-asset volatility is\n  therefore too wide overnight and too narrow at the open, and CRPS charges for both. Measure the\n  seasonality yourself before you model it, and check whether it holds for each asset rather than\n  assuming one shape fits the group.\n\n  Do not \"repair\" NaN gaps by interpolating; scoring already drops NaN per prompt, and inventing\n  prices in the gaps is how you fool your own validation.\n\n  **Fitting vs comparing**: the FULL price history is yours to fit, train and calibrate on. The\n  2026-06-23 configuration split (breaks table below) constrains only FIELD-RELATIVE numbers —\n  never compute one on a window that starts before it. CRPS itself is unaffected.\n\n  **Three structural breaks in the subnet's history. Do not backtest across them:**\n\n  | Date | What changed |\n  |---|---|\n  | 2026-06-23 | The subnet split into the 3 current competitions. Before it: 2 profiles, and COM_EQU_24H did not exist. A field-relative rank spanning this date mixes incompatible reward structures — `synth_lib` will warn, and the number it gives you is meaningless. |\n  | 2026-03-11 | The crypto-1h CRPS formula changed. Ranks before it are not comparable to today's. |\n  | ~2026-07-17 | SPYX was retired and SP500 introduced as a NEW asset, carrying the HIGHEST coefficient (3.44) in COM_EQU_24H. **SPYX is DEAD**: its feed stopped, the validator no longer prompts or scores it, and no post-mid-July prices exist — it still appears in the pinned competition config, but spend NOTHING on it. SP500 has scored field history from ~2026-07-19 onward; fit on its whole price series, validate its RANK on the scored weeks. |\n\n  **Loader expectations**: synth-lib's strict `load_range` RAISES on any NaN or missing day in\n  the requested range — that is a feature for scoring, a trap for exploration. `agent/predict.py`\n  shows the sanctioned lenient pattern; prior agents each rediscovered this at their own expense.\n\n  **Always run your commands from the workspace root**: synth-lib's backtester resolves the\n  price store relative to the current directory (`MinutePriceStore(asset)` with no root, see\n  `_price_store`). From a subfolder it would find nothing and would try to download the missing\n  days — which the read-only mount refuses. `predict.py` already forces this directory and\n  checks coverage before scoring; if you write your own evaluation script, reproduce these two\n  safeguards.\n- **synth-lib** (installed; public source: github.com/synthdataco/synth-lib): the official\n  backtester — `from synth_lib.backtester.orchestration import backtest` — which computes CRPS with\n  the validator's exact CRPS function, and can also place you among the recorded field. Use it\n  via `agent/predict.py`; NEVER reimplement the metric. The backtester runs FULLY OFFLINE here:\n  `SYNTH_BACKTESTER_OFFLINE_DATA_ROOT` is set in your environment and points at a bundle of\n  historical scores inside your snapshot, so no API call is needed. DATA.md gives the measured\n  span of scored prompts it holds: a field-relative number outside that span has nothing to\n  compare against, though CRPS is still computable. DATA.md also gives the span of the cached\n  realized paths, which are a different thing — they stand in for minutes your price store is\n  missing.\n- **synth-subnet** (a synth-lib dependency; source: github.com/synthdataco/synth-subnet): the\n  validator's code. Read it to understand exactly how you'll be scored.\n- **Public Synth API** (api.synthdata.co): historical field scores\n  (`/validation/scores/historical`, query windows ≤ 2 days) and rewards (`/rewards/scores`).\n  synth-lib handles pagination for you. Studying the field (CRPS levels, what the top miners do)\n  is ALLOWED and recommended; copying the paths served by an individual miner is\n  FORBIDDEN.\n- **Miner dashboard analytics API** (`https://monitoring.synthdata.co/v1`, schema at\n  `https://monitoring.synthdata.co/v1/openapi.json`): the public backend behind the miner\n  dashboard — scoreboards, leaderboards, reward pools. Read the OpenAPI schema to discover the\n  endpoints. Same rule as the scores API: studying the field is encouraged, copying an\n  individual miner's served paths is not.\n- **Pretrained models & Hugging Face**: allowed and in scope. The sandbox has network access to\n  the HF Hub and PyPI; the GPU is yours and sits idle unless you use it. Time-series foundation\n  models, fine-tuning, or classical ML are all legitimate strategies — as is deciding, with\n  evidence, that they don't improve on closed-form calibration. If you go this way: download weights\n  EARLY (bandwidth is part of your clock), set `HF_HOME` inside your workspace, and remember\n  your champion must run OFFLINE — weights must be committed to your git workspace, total under\n  a few GB (the harness collects your repo as a git bundle).\n- **Serving latency**: the live validator gives you roughly the prompt's lead time (~1 minute)\n  to respond — that is the hard bound. But the evaluation of your champion generates several\n  thousand prompts, and your own backtests will too: at more than a few seconds per prompt\n  (1000 paths, this hardware), your iteration loop and your evaluation both become the\n  bottleneck. Measure your per-prompt latency early and treat ~5 s as the practical ceiling.\n- **The web**: open for knowledge — searching the literature (arXiv, papers, reference\n  implementations) is encouraged — but NEVER for fetching prices or market data (see hard\n  rules).\n\n## Your envelope\n\n- Credit budget: 100.0 USD. Deadline: 18 h of wall-clock time.\n- The `BUDGET.md` file in your workspace is maintained by the harness: READ IT AT THE START OF\n  EACH ITERATION. When it contains a LANDING ORDER, it is non-negotiable.\n- Hardware: GCP g2-standard-16: 16 vCPU / 64GB RAM / 1x NVIDIA L4 (24GB VRAM). Your sandbox gets 14 CPU, 48GB RAM and the GPU. torch is already in your pyproject — no need to add it.. ONLY ONE GPU job at a time; cap the memory of your heavy jobs.\n- **You are expected to use your envelope.** Iterating is the job: measure, change one thing,\n  measure again. **Nominating is final** — the first time you stop while `agent/CHAMPION`\n  exists, your campaign is over: you will not be resumed, and every remaining dollar and hour\n  is forfeited. Until then you may keep iterating and amend your nomination. Nominate early\n  only when your journal contains EVIDENCE that further iteration would not improve your Score.\n  **A champion is MANDATORY.** What is scored is how strong your champion is, not whether you dared\n  to nominate one: ending with no `agent/CHAMPION` is a Score of 0 and a wasted leg — strictly worse\n  than nominating something imperfect.\n\n## Constraints (they are what make the result valid)\n\n1. **No-lookahead**: a prediction at `start_time` uses only information available at\n   `start_time`. Your champion is evaluated on the 10 days FOLLOWING your data cutoff: treat\n   everything after **2026-09-14** as nonexistent. Querying ANY API (scores, rewards,\n   dashboard) for times after 2026-09-14 is lookahead into your own evaluation window and\n   invalidates the result. Audited on your transcript.\n2. **Prices = snapshot only.** No fetching market data online (price APIs, exchanges,\n   aggregators). The web is for knowledge only. Audited on your transcript.\n3. **Scoring = synth-lib.** Every CRPS evaluation goes through `synth_lib.backtester`\n   (via `agent/predict.py`). One implementation of the metric is what makes results comparable.\n4. Not allowed: falsifying the journal or results; reproducing another participant's served\n   output as your own.\n5. **Keep binary and encoded data out of your terminal output.** Read parquet with pandas and\n   `.npy` with numpy — never `cat` a parquet, a git bundle, or model weights, and never pipe\n   base64 into your context. A screenful of encoded bytes buys you nothing, costs context, and\n   is a known trigger for content classifiers that will end your turn.\n6. **Start from public sources only**: synth-lib, synth-subnet, the Synth API, and your own\n   ideas. The operator's existing miner models are not part of your materials and must not\n   inform your work. Nothing of that kind belongs in your environment; if any of it turns up\n   there, leave it unread and note it in `suggestions.md`.\n7. Your final champion must expose, in `agent/modeling.py`:\n   `simulate(asset, start_time, time_increment, time_length, num_simulations, context_prices)`\n   -> tuple `(start_timestamp, time_increment, *paths)` — each path: a list of\n   `time_length//time_increment + 1` floats starting at the last price in the context.\n   **The `context_prices` contract, exactly**: a minute-indexed pd.DataFrame over the 7 days\n   preceding `start_time`, with columns `open, high, low, close, volume, trade_count`. `close`\n   is the series you are scored against; the rest are context. It MAY CONTAIN NaN in every\n   column — your `simulate()` must tolerate them (drop, never interpolate). Evaluation passes\n   the raw frame: handle gaps inside your model, not in a loader you hope evaluation shares.\n   Runnable OFFLINE (weights/artifacts committed or included in the workspace).\n8. **If your model uses randomness at all, seed it from `SYNTH_BENCHMARK_SEED`.** Nothing\n   here asks you to introduce randomness — a deterministic champion ignores this rule. If you\n   do use it, read the variable once at the top of `simulate()` and seed every generator your\n   model touches from it: Python's `random`, NumPy, torch, and anything carrying its own. It\n   is absent during your own runs and set only at evaluation, so fall back to a seed of your\n   choosing when it is missing. What you may never do is hardcode a seed that ignores the\n   variable, or leave a generator unseeded so the clock or OS entropy supplies it.\n\n## Required deliverables (in `agent/`)\n\nWrite `journal.md`, `report.md`, and `suggestions.md` in English — these reports are published\nto the Synth miner community.\n\n- `journal.md`: one entry per iteration — Hypothesis / Change / Result (numbers) / Verdict\n  (keep-discard) / Lesson. As you go: this is your memory if you crash.\n- `report.md`: your final report, explainer style (audience = Synth miners). Sections: context,\n  approach, experiments (failures included), results, limitations, what you'd do with more\n  budget.\n- `CHAMPION`: YAML — `sha` (an EXISTING git commit in your workspace, not a placeholder),\n  `agent_dir: agent`, `profiles`, `notes`. `profiles` must list the formats your champion\n  supports: `[low, high]` if you cover both 24h AND 1h (expected), `[low]` if you could only\n  handle the 24h. `notes` MUST include your champion's backtested **CRPS per configuration**,\n  produced by the official backtester (`agent/predict.py`) at 1000 paths, alongside the CRPS of\n  whatever you started from — a nomination without measured numbers is incomplete. The harness checks the interface as soon as\n  this file appears (result in BUDGET.md) — fix it if the check fails.\n- `suggestions.md`: anything that falls outside your scope.\n- you must commit all deliverables to your workspace git repo before the deadline.\n\n## Method (your choice, but recommended)\n\nYou choose your own research questions, hypotheses, splits, and criteria. What works here:\nwrite the hypothesis BEFORE the experiment, pre-registered kill thresholds, validation on 2\nwindows of opposite regimes (within the limits of the snapshot), one variable at a time,\nfrequent commits. Watch out for the edges of eval windows: only backtest windows fully covered\nby the snapshot.\n\n## Resuming after a crash\n\nIf you restart with no context: reread this file, then `BUDGET.md`, then your `journal.md`,\nand continue where you left off.\n","numLines":262,"startLine":1,"totalLines":262}}}
