the *conditional* variance of the\n   log volatility forecast. I would have saved two hours by knowing it at the start.\n\n9. **The prompt anchor is stale by design.** Context ends at `start − (ISSUANCE_LEAD + 60)` s\n   while `real_prices[0]` is the close of the bar labelled `start` — 180 s of unobserved drift for\n   24h prompts, 120 s for 1h. The relative-change intervals cancel it; only `24hour_abs` (3.6 %\n   of the budget) sees it, and only as ~0.1 % extra dispersion. Correctly not worth modelling,\n   but it is not obvious from the code and took a while to convince myself of.\n\n## Things I would try next\n\n10. **Cross-asset volatility pooling.** BTC/ETH/SOL/XRP/HYPE share a common vol factor; the thin\n    equities share the US cash session. Estimating a common factor plus an idiosyncratic loading\n    should beat five independent EWMAs, especially for the assets whose own history is thin. This\n    is the most promising untried idea — it attacks the residual directly.\n\n11. **A proper GARCH/HAR likelihood fit per asset**, rather than EWMA half-lives chosen by sweep.\n    My regression attempt (Iter 6) failed out of sample, but it was fitted on a squared-log-error\n    objective; fitting the *CRPS* objective directly, with the asymmetry in §8 respected, is a\n    different and better-posed problem.\n\n12. **Per-step calibration of the com-equ multiplier.** The eight fitted multipliers are constants,\n    but the thing they are correcting — the ratio between the RMS the estimator sees and the\n    width CRPS wants — is almost certainly a function of how dead the market is at that step.\n    A multiplier that is a function of the seasonal profile value would generalise better than\n    one number per asset.\n\n13. **Tick/volume features.** `trade_count` and `volume` are in the context and I never used\n    them. For thin assets, trade count is the most direct measure of whether the next five\n    minutes can move at all, and it is exactly the regime my const