{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01X9kUTsMy7XnmQtqxigWQCM","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Forecasting price distributions for Synth SN50: what moved the CRPS and what didn't\n2\t\n3\t*A campaign report for Synth miners. Everything here was derived from the public snapshot, the\n4\tsynth-lib backtester and the Synth API; no proprietary model informed it.*\n5\t\n6\t---\n7\t\n8\t## 1. The problem, stated precisely\n9\t\n10\tA Synth validator hands you an asset, a start time, a horizon and a step size, and asks for 1000\n11\tsimulated price paths. It scores you with CRPS — lower is better. Three configurations are live:\n12\t\n13\t| configuration | assets | horizon / step | points |\n14\t|---|---|---|---|\n15\t| CRYPTO_24H | BTC ETH SOL XRP HYPE | 86400 s / 300 s | 289 |\n16\t| COM_EQU_24H | XAU NVDAX GOOGLX TSLAX AAPLX WTIOIL SPCX SP500 | 86400 s / 300 s | 289 |\n17\t| CRYPTO_1H | BTC ETH SOL XRP HYPE | 3600 s / 60 s | 61 |\n18\t\n19\tThe first thing worth knowing is what the score actually is, because it is not \"how realistic are\n20\tyour paths\".\n21\t\n22\t**The validator score is a sum of *univariate* CRPS terms on relative price changes over\n23\tintervals.** For a 24-hour prompt:\n24\t\n25\t| interval | number of terms | share of the score |\n26\t|---|---|---|\n27\t| 5 min | 288 | ≈ 61 % |\n28\t| 30 min | 48 | ≈ 25 % |\n29\t| 3 hour | 8 | ≈ 10 % |\n30\t| 24 hour (absolute) | 1 | ≈ 3.6 % |\n31\t\n32\tThere is no multivariate term anywhere. The joint distribution of a path matters *only* through\n33\tthe marginals of aggregated returns. Roughly 86 % of a 24-hour score is the marginal distribution\n34\tof the 5-minute return at each of 288 future steps.\n35\t\n36\tThis has a blunt consequence: cross-asset correlation, jump clustering, order-flow realism,\n37\tmomentum, and every other thing that makes a simulated path look like a real one are **unpriced**.\n38\tIf you are spending effort there, you are spending it on something the scoring function cannot\n39\tsee.\n40\t\n41\tCRYPTO_1H is similar in spirit: 1/2/5/15/30/60-minute intervals, plus twelve `*_gaps` terms\n42\t(cumulative return from the start to each of minutes 5, 10, …, 60) worth ≈29 %, plus a small\n43\tvolatility-CRPS block worth ~1 %.\n44\t\n45\t## 2. The identity that decides everything\n46\t\n47\tBecause the score is a sum of scale-family CRPS terms, you can write down what a mis-calibrated\n48\twidth costs. For a Gaussian forecast of scale σ against a truth of scale 1:\n49\t\n50\t```\n51\tE CRPS(σ) = sqrt(2/π)·sqrt(1+σ²) − σ/sqrt(π)\n52\t```\n53\t\n54\tminimised at σ = 1 with value 1/sqrt(π). Expanding around the optimum:\n55\t\n56\t```\n57\tE CRPS(σ) / E CRPS(1)  ≈  1 + 0.25 · (log σ)²\n58\t```\n59\t\n60\tI measured this empirically before deriving it, and the two agree to two digits: a **±20 % error\n61\tin overall scale costs about 1 % of CRPS**, and a 5 % error costs 0.06 %.\n62\t\n63\tRead that the other way round and it is the whole campaign in one line:\n64\t\n65\t> A *constant* scale error is nearly free. The entire recoverable CRPS is\n66\t> **Var(log σ̂ − log σ_true)** — the variance of your volatility forecast error in logs.\n67\t\n68\tIt even predicts the size of the prize. For BTC, sd(log realized 24-hour vol) ≈ 0.48, so a model\n69\twith a constant width gives up 0.25 × 0.48² ≈ 5.8 %. An oracle handed the realized volatility of\n70\tthe very window it is predicting scored ≈6 % better than my best causal model on crypto-24h.\n71\tThe arithmetic and the experiment agree.\n72\t\n73\tOne asymmetry matters in practice: the quadratic is symmetric but the exact loss is not. Too\n74\t*wide* costs linearly and without bound; too *narrow* saturates at +41 %. So the CRPS-optimal\n75\tforecast sits slightly *below* `exp(E[log σ])`, which is why every multiplier I fitted came out\n76\tunder 1.\n77\t\n78\t## 3. The model\n79\t\n80\t```\n81\tr_k = scale(asset) · v_k · s(t_k) · z ,        price_k = last · exp(cumsum(r))\n82\t```\n83\t\n84\t* **`s(t_k)` — time-of-week seasonality.** A 336-bucket (half-hourly) profile per asset and step\n85\t  size, fitted offline and committed as an artifact. The venues never close but the underlying\n86\t  markets do: mean |5-minute return| swings ≈1.8× across the week for crypto and up to ≈6× for\n87\t  the tokenised equities. The profile is raised to the power 0.85 — damping it beat both the raw\n88\t  profile and no profile at all.\n89\t* **`v_k` — conditional volatility of the *deseasonalised* returns.** A geometric blend of EWMA\n90\t  variances at half-lives of 1 h / 6 h / 48 h, reverting log-linearly toward the 7-day level with\n91\t  a half-life of 96 steps.\n92\t* **`z` — standardised Student-t(4).** Gaussian innovations cost about 1 %.\n93\t* **`scale(asset)`** — 0.90 for every crypto asset; one fitted multiplier each for the eight\n94\t  com-equ assets (0.72–0.88).\n95\t\n96\tOne configuration serves all three scoring configurations. It runs offline in ~40 ms per prompt\n97\tat 1000 paths, against a practical ceiling of about 5 s.\n98\t\n99\tEverything in the model is there because it survived a check on a validation window it was not\n100\ttuned on. Most of what I tried did not.\n101\t\n102\t## 4. What worked\n103\t\n104\t### Time-of-week seasonality (−1.9 %) — the largest single win\n105\t\n106\tVolatility in these markets is strongly deterministic in time-of-week, and a constant σ is\n107\tsimultaneously too wide overnight and too narrow at the US cash open. CRPS charges for both.\n108\tDamped at γ = 0.85 this is worth −1.9 % across 13 assets, concentrated in com-equ (NVDAX −3.5 %,\n109\tTSLAX −3.8 %, GOOGLX −3.2 %).\n110\t\n111\t### Student-t(4) innovations (−1 %)\n112\t\n113\tEssentially the entire prize available from distribution *shape*. Swapping t(4) for a normal at\n114\tidentical scale costs ~1 %; `nu=3` and `nu=6` are both worse; an empirical bootstrap of the\n115\tcontext returns is a wash.\n116\t\n117\t### Volatility mean reversion (−0.6 %)\n118\t\n119\tReverting the current EWMA level toward the 7-day level with a 96-step half-life. This was the\n120\t*only* one of the vol-dynamics knobs that mattered — see §5.\n121\t\n122\t### A per-asset width multiplier for com-equ (−0.13 % out of sample)\n123\t\n124\tFitted on a window preceding both validation windows. Small, but it is the part of the per-asset\n125\tcalibration that demonstrably generalises, and the diagnosis is the interesting bit (§5).\n126\t\n127\t## 5. What didn't work — the more useful half\n128\t\n129\t### Median-based seasonality: +10 % on XAU\n130\t\n131\tMy first seasonality profile used the bucket **median** |return| and made things dramatically\n132\tworse. In a thin market's quiet hours most 5-minute returns are *exactly zero* — no trade — so\n133\tthe median collapses toward 0 while the width CRPS actually wants does not. The model became\n134\tabsurdly narrow overnight and was punished for it.\n135\t\n136\tSwitching to a **winsorised RMS** (clipped at 6 robust sigmas; XRP's raw return kurtosis on this\n137\tsnapshot is about 7000, so a plain RMS is set by single prints) turned a +0.35 % regression into\n138\ta −1.9 % improvement.\n139\t\n140\t> When a feature makes things worse, the feature is usually right and the *estimator* is wrong.\n141\t> And in this data, \"no trade\" and \"no move\" are the same observation.\n142\t\n143\t### A fitted volatility regression: better forecast, worse score\n144\t\n145\t§2 says the prize is Var(log σ̂ − log σ). That is a regression target, so I regressed it: log\n146\tfuture volatility on the log EWMA vols of the deseasonalised context at five half-lives, per\n147\tasset, at five horizon knots, so the fitted cumulative-variance curve could be differentiated\n148\tinto a per-step term structure.\n149\t\n150\tIn-sample R² was 0.36–0.73. Measured against realized volatility it *was* a better forecast than\n151\tthe EWMA blend on crypto (log-error sd 0.281 vs 0.314 for BTC). It still lost on CRPS, and it\n152\ttook three separate fixes to even find out why:\n153\t\n154\t1. **`np.interp` clamps below its first knot.** The cumulative variance curve came out flat over\n155\t   the first steps — literally zero dispersion at steps 1 and 2. CRPS punishes a zero-width\n156\t   forecast savagely.\n157\t2. **The fitted \"term structure\" was almost entirely small-sample log bias.** The target is\n158\t   `log(RMS of h returns)` and log is concave, so `E[log RMS₃]` sits ~45 % below `log σ` for\n159\t   fat-tailed returns while `E[log RMS₂₈₈]` is within 0.3 %. Fitting each horizon separately\n160\t   manufactures a steep upward slope that is pure estimator artefact. The fix is to measure the\n161\t   bias on non-overlapping blocks of the same series and subtract it.\n162\t3. **The A/B test was not testing what I thought.** With a 96-step reversion half-life over a\n163\t   288-step path, most of the path has already reverted to the long-run level, so \"swapping in\n164\t   the regression\" barely used the regression.\n165\t\n166\tAfter all three fixes, at each model's individually optimal multiplier the fitted and EWMA levels\n167\t**tie** (mean excess loss 3.40 % vs 3.37 %). Out of sample on the later window, the regression was\n168\tworse on exactly the assets it was supposed to help: HYPE +1.12 %, XRP +0.74 %, SOL +0.53 %.\n169\t\n170\tRejected. The code stays in the repo as a *measurement instrument* — its residual sd is what tells\n171\tme 3.6 % (24h) and 2.5 % (1h) of CRPS remain on the table.\n172\t\n173\t### Almost every volatility-dynamics knob\n174\t\n175\tSwept `revert_halflife` ∈ {24,48,96,192,384}, three half-life triples, three weight vectors,\n176\tvol-of-vol ∈ {0.2,0.35,0.5}, winsorisation ∈ {2.5,3.5,6}, and an L1 variance estimator. Only\n177\t`revert_halflife` moved the mean by more than 0.1 %. This is exactly what §2 predicts: these knobs\n178\tmove the *level*, and the level is nearly free.\n179\t\n180\t### Per-asset scales for crypto\n181\t\n182\tThe same calibration that produced good com-equ multipliers produced crypto multipliers that were\n183\tnoise. Mapping all 13 assets gave −0.21 % on the first window and **+0.13 % on the second**;\n184\tcom-equ only gave −0.21 % and −0.13 %.\n185\t\n186\tWhat separated them was *stability under refitting*. Refitted four months apart on disjoint data,\n187\tthe com-equ multipliers barely moved — GOOGLX 0.746 → 0.741, NVDAX 0.761 → 0.755, SPCX 0.831 →\n188\t0.835, XAU 0.764 → 0.773 — while the crypto ones wandered (HYPE 1.074 → 0.984). Parameter\n189\tstability under refitting predicted out-of-sample transfer better than in-sample gain did.\n190\t\n191\t### A bespoke crypto-1h model\n192\t\n193\tA 60-minute horizon at 1-minute resolution *ought* to want faster volatility dynamics. It does\n194\tnot: the (2,10,60)-minute blend is 0.41 % worse than (12,72,576). Slower half-lives, a 7-day pool\n195\tand empirical innovations together looked worth −0.43 % on the first window and delivered nothing\n196\ton the second (all variants within ±0.08 %, the unchanged configuration marginally ahead). So\n197\tcrypto-1h ships with the 24-hour settings untouched.\n198\t\n199\t### Return term-structure correction\n200\t\n201\tEmpirical variance ratios are below 1 (BTC 0.92 at 5-hour horizons, AAPLX 0.67), so an i.i.d.\n202\tmodel over-disperses the 30-minute and 3-hour blocks. Matching them needs an MA(1) coefficient of\n203\tabout −0.04, which corrects a ~4 % scale error on 39 % of the budget. By §2 that is worth\n204\t0.25 × 0.04² ≈ 0.04 % of those terms. Computed, not implemented — the identity in §2 is very good\n205\tat telling you what *not* to build.\n206\t\n207\t## 6. Results\n208\t\n209\tBacktested with `agent/predict.py` (the official `synth_lib.backtester`), 7 days of prompts ending\n210\t2026-09-13, 1000 simulated paths — the same count the validator serves.\n211\t\n212\t<!--RESULTS-->\n213\t\n214\tFor scale: over 2026-07-10→12, the *best miner in the live field* on BTC scored 1841.4, with a\n215\tmedian of 1997 and p75 of 2016 across ~200 miners — many of them identical to within 0.1, which\n216\tsuggests a widely shared template. The starting-point model in this repo is not competitive with\n217\tthat; the champion is well inside it.\n218\t\n219\t## 7. Limitations\n220\t\n221\t* **The evaluation window is 10 days of live trading that I cannot see.** Every number here is a\n222\t  backtest. The com-equ multipliers are stable across four months of history, but a volatility\n223\t  regime change in the evaluation window would hurt com-equ more than crypto.\n224\t* **The seasonality artifact is fitted on the whole snapshot**, so the backtested numbers in §6\n225\t  are mildly optimistic relative to a truly causal run. The validation numbers quoted throughout\n226\t  §4–§5 all use artifacts fitted strictly before their window, and the effects survive there.\n227\t* **Two tokenised equities (SPCX, SP500) have short histories** — SP500 only from the mid-July\n228\t  SPYX retirement — so their profiles and multipliers rest on less data than the others.\n229\t* **The model has no drift and no cross-asset structure.** Both are defensible given §1, but the\n230\t  second is the most promising thing I left on the table (§8).\n231\t* **`num_simulations` is honoured as passed**, but everything was tuned at 1000. Empirical CRPS is\n232\t  biased upward at small sample sizes; at 100 paths the absolute numbers would be worse, though\n233\t  the ranking between variants was stable in spot checks.\n234\t\n235\t## 8. What I would do with more budget\n236\t\n237\t1. **Cross-asset volatility pooling.** BTC/ETH/SOL/XRP/HYPE share a common volatility factor and\n238\t   the tokenised equities share the US cash session. A common factor plus per-asset loadings\n239\t   should beat 13 independent EWMAs — most of all for the assets whose own history is thin. This\n240\t   attacks the residual variance directly, which §2 says is the only thing that pays.\n241\t2. **Fit the CRPS objective, not a squared-log-error proxy.** My regression minimised the wrong\n242\t   loss and respected neither the asymmetry in §2 nor the seasonal weighting. The CRPS-optimal\n243\t   forecast is a different, better-posed estimation problem.\n244\t3. **Use `volume` and `trade_count`.** They sit unused in the context. For a thin asset, trade\n245\t   count is the most direct measure of whether the next five minutes *can* move — which is\n246\t   precisely the regime my constant per-asset multiplier is papering over.\n247\t4. **Make the com-equ multiplier a function of the seasonal level** rather than a constant. The\n248\t   thing it corrects (the gap between the RMS the estimator sees and the width CRPS wants) is\n249\t   almost certainly a function of how dead the market is at that step.\n250\t\n251\t## 9. Reproducing this\n252\t\n253\t```bash\n254\tuv run python agent/seasonality.py                  # rebuild the time-of-week artifacts\n255\tuv run python agent/calibrate.py dev                # refit the per-asset multipliers\n256\tuv run agent/predict.py --asset BTC --days 7 --eval-end 2026-09-13 --num-simulations 1000\n257\t```\n258\t\n259\t`agent/harness.py` + `agent/evaluate.py` + `agent/sweep.py` are the fast iteration loop — 545\n260\tprompts × 1000 paths × 5 assets in 16 s, against ~20 s per asset-day for the official path. It\n261\tcomputes CRPS with the very same `calculate_total_score_for_miner` the validator calls, on\n262\trealized paths assembled the way `backtest()` assembles them, and it is cross-checked against\n263\t`predict.py` in `journal.md` (2740.9 vs 2743.4 on an identical prediction file). Every number\n264\tquoted as a *result* still goes through `predict.py`.\n265\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e0fdc998-0d4c-4bec-aa01-713aae481473","uuid":"f3b02313-4dfe-48cb-8217-ddbd273d3db5","timestamp":"2026-09-29T20:03:29.492Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/workspace/agent/report.md","content":"# Forecasting price distributions for Synth SN50: what moved the CRPS and what didn't\n\n*A campaign report for Synth miners. Everything here was derived from the public snapshot, the\nsynth-lib backtester and the Synth API; no proprietary model informed it.*\n\n---\n\n## 1. The problem, stated precisely\n\nA Synth validator hands you an asset, a start time, a horizon and a step size, and asks for 1000\nsimulated price paths. It scores you with CRPS — lower is better. Three configurations are live:\n\n| configuration | assets | horizon / step | points |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| CRYPTO_24H | BTC ETH SOL XRP HYPE | 86400 s / 300 s | 289 |\n| COM_EQU_24H | XAU NVDAX GOOGLX TSLAX AAPLX WTIOIL SPCX SP500 | 86400 s / 300 s | 289 |\n| CRYPTO_1H | BTC ETH SOL XRP HYPE | 3600 s / 60 s | 61 |\n\nThe first thing worth knowing is what the score actually is, because it is not \"how realistic are\nyour paths\".\n\n**The validator score is a sum of *univariate* CRPS terms on relative price changes over\nintervals.** For a 24-hour prompt:\n\n| interval | number of terms | share of the score |\n|---|---|---|\n| 5 min | 288 | ≈ 61 % |\n| 30 min | 48 | ≈ 25 % |\n| 3 hour | 8 | ≈ 10 % |\n| 24 hour (absolute) | 1 | ≈ 3.6 % |\n\nThere is no multivariate term anywhere. The joint distribution of a path matters *only* through\nthe marginals of aggregated returns. Roughly 86 % of a 24-hour score is the marginal distribution\nof the 5-minute return at each of 288 future steps.\n\nThis has a blunt consequence: cross-asset correlation, jump clustering, order-flow realism,\nmomentum, and every other thing that makes a simulated path look like a real one are **unpriced**.\nIf you are spending effort there, you are spending it on something the scoring function cannot\nsee.\n\nCRYPTO_1H is similar in spirit: 1/2/5/15/30/60-minute intervals, plus twelve `*_gaps` terms\n(cumulative return from the start to each of minutes 5, 10, …, 60) worth ≈29 %, plus a small\nvolatility-CRPS block worth ~1 %.\n\n## 2. The identity that decides everything\n\nBecause the score is a sum of scale-family CRPS terms, you can write down what a mis-calibrated\nwidth costs. For a Gaussian forecast of scale σ against a truth of scale 1:\n\n```\nE CRPS(σ) = sqrt(2/π)·sqrt(1+σ²) − σ/sqrt(π)\n```\n\nminimised at σ = 1 with value 1/sqrt(π). Expanding around the optimum:\n\n```\nE CRPS(σ) / E CRPS(1)  ≈  1 + 0.25 · (log σ)²\n```\n\nI measured this empirically before deriving it, and the two agree to two digits: a **±20 % error\nin overall scale costs about 1 % of CRPS**, and a 5 % error costs 0.06 %.\n\nRead that the other way round and it is the whole campaign in one line:\n\n> A *constant* scale error is nearly free. The entire recoverable CRPS is\n> **Var(log σ̂ − log σ_true)** — the variance of your volatility forecast error in logs.\n\nIt even predicts the size of the prize. For BTC, sd(log realized 24-hour vol) ≈ 0.48, so a model\nwith a constant width gives up 0.25 × 0.48² ≈ 5.8 %. An oracle handed the realized volatility of\nthe very window it is predicting scored ≈6 % better than my best causal model on crypto-24h.\nThe arithmetic and the experiment agree.\n\nOne asymmetry matters in practice: the quadratic is symmetric but the exact loss is not. Too\n*wide* costs linearly and without bound; too *narrow* saturates at +41 %. So the CRPS-optimal\nforecast sits slightly *below* `exp(E[log σ])`, which is why every multiplier I fitted came out\nunder 1.\n\n## 3. The model\n\n```\nr_k = scale(asset) · v_k · s(t_k) · z ,        price_k = last · exp(cumsum(r))\n```\n\n* **`s(t_k)` — time-of-week seasonality.** A 336-bucket (half-hourly) profile per asset and step\n  size, fitted offline and committed as an artifact. The venues never close but the underlying\n  markets do: mean |5-minute return| swings ≈1.8× across the week for crypto and up to ≈6× for\n  the tokenised equities. The profile is raised to the power 0.85 — damping it beat both the raw\n  profile and no profile at all.\n* **`v_k` — conditional volatility of the *deseasonalised* returns.** A geometric blend of EWMA\n  variances at half-lives of 1 h / 6 h / 48 h, reverting log-linearly toward the 7-day level with\n  a half-life of 96 steps.\n* **`z` — standardised Student-t(4).** Gaussian innovations cost about 1 %.\n* **`scale(asset)`** — 0.90 for every crypto asset; one fitted multiplier each for the eight\n  com-equ assets (0.72–0.88).\n\nOne configuration serves all three scoring configurations. It runs offline in ~40 ms per prompt\nat 1000 paths, against a practical ceiling of about 5 s.\n\nEverything in the model is there because it survived a check on a validation window it was not\ntuned on. Most of what I tried did not.\n\n## 4. What worked\n\n### Time-of-week seasonality (−1.9 %) — the largest single win\n\nVolatility in these markets is strongly deterministic in time-of-week, and a constant σ is\nsimultaneously too wide overnight and too narrow at the US cash open. CRPS charges for both.\nDamped at γ = 0.85 this is worth −1.9 % across 13 assets, concentrated in com-equ (NVDAX −3.5 %,\nTSLAX −3.8 %, GOOGLX −3.2 %).\n\n### Student-t(4) innovations (−1 %)\n\nEssentially the entire prize available from distribution *shape*. Swapping t(4) for a normal at\nidentical scale costs ~1 %; `nu=3` and `nu=6` are both worse; an empirical bootstrap of the\ncontext returns is a wash.\n\n### Volatility mean reversion (−0.6 %)\n\nReverting the current EWMA level toward the 7-day level with a 96-step half-life. This was the\n*only* one of the vol-dynamics knobs that mattered — see §5.\n\n### A per-asset width multiplier for com-equ (−0.13 % out of sample)\n\nFitted on a window preceding both validation windows. Small, but it is the part of the per-asset\ncalibration that demonstrably generalises, and the diagnosis is the interesting bit (§5).\n\n## 5. What didn't work — the more useful half\n\n### Median-based seasonality: +10 % on XAU\n\nMy first seasonality profile used the bucket **median** |return| and made things dramatically\nworse. In a thin market's quiet hours most 5-minute returns are *exactly zero* — no trade — so\nthe median collapses toward 0 while the width CRPS actually wants does not. The model became\nabsurdly narrow overnight and was punished for it.\n\nSwitching to a **winsorised RMS** (clipped at 6 robust sigmas; XRP's raw return kurtosis on this\nsnapshot is about 7000, so a plain RMS is set by single prints) turned a +0.35 % regression into\na −1.9 % improvement.\n\n> When a feature makes things worse, the feature is usually right and the *estimator* is wrong.\n> And in this data, \"no trade\" and \"no move\" are the same observation.\n\n### A fitted volatility regression: better forecast, worse score\n\n§2 says the prize is Var(log σ̂ − log σ). That is a regression target, so I regressed it: log\nfuture volatility on the log EWMA vols of the deseasonalised context at five half-lives, per\nasset, at five horizon knots, so the fitted cumulative-variance curve could be differentiated\ninto a per-step term structure.\n\nIn-sample R² was 0.36–0.73. Measured against realized volatility it *was* a better forecast than\nthe EWMA blend on crypto (log-error sd 0.281 vs 0.314 for BTC). It still lost on CRPS, and it\ntook three separate fixes to even find out why:\n\n1. **`np.interp` clamps below its first knot.** The cumulative variance curve came out flat over\n   the first steps — literally zero dispersion at steps 1 and 2. CRPS punishes a zero-width\n   forecast savagely.\n2. **The fitted \"term structure\" was almost entirely small-sample log bias.** The target is\n   `log(RMS of h returns)` and log is concave, so `E[log RMS₃]` sits ~45 % below `log σ` for\n   fat-tailed returns while `E[log RMS₂₈₈]` is within 0.3 %. Fitting each horizon separately\n   manufactures a steep upward slope that is pure estimator artefact. The fix is to measure the\n   bias on non-overlapping blocks of the same series and subtract it.\n3. **The A/B test was not testing what I thought.** With a 96-step reversion half-life over a\n   288-step path, most of the path has already reverted to the long-run level, so \"swapping in\n   the regression\" barely used the regression.\n\nAfter all three fixes, at each model's individually optimal multiplier the fitted and EWMA levels\n**tie** (mean excess loss 3.40 % vs 3.37 %). Out of sample on the later window, the regression was\nworse on exactly the assets it was supposed to help: HYPE +1.12 %, XRP +0.74 %, SOL +0.53 %.\n\nRejected. The code stays in the repo as a *measurement instrument* — its residual sd is what tells\nme 3.6 % (24h) and 2.5 % (1h) of CRPS remain on the table.\n\n### Almost every volatility-dynamics knob\n\nSwept `revert_halflife` ∈ {24,48,96,192,384}, three half-life triples, three weight vectors,\nvol-of-vol ∈ {0.2,0.35,0.5}, winsorisation ∈ {2.5,3.5,6}, and an L1 variance estimator. Only\n`revert_halflife` moved the mean by more than 0.1 %. This is exactly what §2 predicts: these knobs\nmove the *level*, and the level is nearly free.\n\n### Per-asset scales for crypto\n\nThe same calibration that produced good com-equ multipliers produced crypto multipliers that were\nnoise. Mapping all 13 assets gave −0.21 % on the first window and **+0.13 % on the second**;\ncom-equ only gave −0.21 % and −0.13 %.\n\nWhat separated them was *stability under refitting*. Refitted four months apart on disjoint data,\nthe com-equ multipliers barely moved — GOOGLX 0.746 → 0.741, NVDAX 0.761 → 0.755, SPCX 0.831 →\n0.835, XAU 0.764 → 0.773 — while the crypto ones wandered (HYPE 1.074 → 0.984). Parameter\nstability under refitting predicted out-of-sample transfer better than in-sample gain did.\n\n### A bespoke crypto-1h model\n\nA 60-minute horizon at 1-minute resolution *ought* to want faster volatility dynamics. It does\nnot: the (2,10,60)-minute blend is 0.41 % worse than (12,72,576). Slower half-lives, a 7-day pool\nand empirical innovations together looked worth −0.43 % on the first window and delivered nothing\non the second (all variants within ±0.08 %, the unchanged configuration marginally ahead). So\ncrypto-1h ships with the 24-hour settings untouched.\n\n### Return term-structure correction\n\nEmpirical variance ratios are below 1 (BTC 0.92 at 5-hour horizons, AAPLX 0.67), so an i.i.d.\nmodel over-disperses the 30-minute and 3-hour blocks. Matching them needs an MA(1) coefficient of\nabout −0.04, which corrects a ~4 % scale error on 39 % of the budget. By §2 that is worth\n0.25 × 0.04² ≈ 0.04 % of those terms. Computed, not implemented — the identity in §2 is very good\nat telling you what *not* to build.\n\n## 6. Results\n\nBacktested with `agent/predict.py` (the official `synth_lib.backtester`), 7 days of prompts ending\n2026-09-13, 1000 simulated paths — the same count the validator serves.\n\n<!--RESULTS-->\n\nFor scale: over 2026-07-10→12, the *best miner in the live field* on BTC scored 1841.4, with a\nmedian of 1997 and p75 of 2016 across ~200 miners — many of them identical to within 0.1, which\nsuggests a widely shared template. The starting-point model in this repo is not competitive with\nthat; the champion is well inside it.\n\n## 7. Limitations\n\n* **The evaluation window is 10 days of live trading that I cannot see.** Every number here is a\n  backtest. The com-equ multipliers are stable across four months of history, but a volatility\n  regime change in the evaluation window would hurt com-equ more than crypto.\n* **The seasonality artifact is fitted on the whole snapshot**, so the backtested numbers in §6\n  are mildly optimistic relative to a truly causal run. The validation numbers quoted throughout\n  §4–§5 all use artifacts fitted strictly before their window, and the effects survive there.\n* **Two tokenised equities (SPCX, SP500) have short histories** — SP500 only from the mid-July\n  SPYX retirement — so their profiles and multipliers rest on less data than the others.\n* **The model has no drift and no cross-asset structure.** Both are defensible given §1, but the\n  second is the most promising thing I left on the table (§8).\n* **`num_simulations` is honoured as passed**, but everything was tuned at 1000. Empirical CRPS is\n  biased upward at small sample sizes; at 100 paths the absolute numbers would be worse, though\n  the ranking between variants was stable in spot checks.\n\n## 8. What I would do with more budget\n\n1. **Cross-asset volatility pooling.** BTC/ETH/SOL/XRP/HYPE share a common volatility factor and\n   the tokenised equities share the US cash session. A common factor plus per-asset loadings\n   should beat 13 independent EWMAs — most of all for the assets whose own history is thin. This\n   attacks the residual variance directly, which §2 says is the only thing that pays.\n2. **Fit the CRPS objective, not a squared-log-error proxy.** My regression minimised the wrong\n   loss and respected neither the asymmetry in §2 nor the seasonal weighting. The CRPS-optimal\n   forecast is a different, better-posed estimation problem.\n3. **Use `volume` and `trade_count`.** They sit unused in the context. For a thin asset, trade\n   count is the most direct measure of whether the next five minutes *can* move — which is\n   precisely the regime my constant per-asset multiplier is papering over.\n4. **Make the com-equ multiplier a function of the seasonal level** rather than a constant. The\n   thing it corrects (the gap between the RMS the estimator sees and the width CRPS wants) is\n   almost certainly a function of how dead the market is at that step.\n\n## 9. Reproducing this\n\n```bash\nuv run python agent/seasonality.py                  # rebuild the time-of-week artifacts\nuv run python agent/calibrate.py dev                # refit the per-asset multipliers\nuv run agent/predict.py --asset BTC --days 7 --eval-end 2026-09-13 --num-simulations 1000\n```\n\n`agent/harness.py` + `agent/evaluate.py` + `agent/sweep.py` are the fast iteration loop — 545\nprompts × 1000 paths × 5 assets in 16 s, against ~20 s per asset-day for the official path. It\ncomputes CRPS with the very same `calculate_total_score_for_miner` the validator calls, on\nrealized paths assembled the way `backtest()` assembles them, and it is cross-checked against\n`predict.py` in `journal.md` (2740.9 vs 2743.4 on an identical prediction file). Every number\nquoted as a *result* still goes through `predict.py`.\n","numLines":265,"startLine":1,"totalLines":265}}}
