{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01R7EshzQTtizBRWrRjp44Fc","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /workspace/agent/suggestions.md has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e0fdc998-0d4c-4bec-aa01-713aae481473","uuid":"65b465f0-4f55-4b75-9884-4c5bf8ce1d18","timestamp":"2026-09-29T20:21:08.307Z","tool_use_result":{"filePath":"/workspace/agent/suggestions.md","oldString":"## On the scoring rule","newString":"A3. **The backtester reaches 26 hours past the last prompt, and a missing day partition triggers a\n   network ingest into a read-only mount.** `backtest()` step 5 asks `download_price_data` for\n   `[min(start_time) − 2h, max(start_time) + 26h]`; that helper walks the range one *day* at a\n   time and sends any absent partition to `MinutePriceStore.ingest_range`. With a snapshot that\n   ends 2026-09-14 this is invisible for 24-hour prompts (the latest scorable one starts 09-12,\n   so +26 h stays inside) and fatal for 1-hour prompts (the latest starts 09-13 22:00, so +26 h\n   lands on 09-15): all five crypto-1h assets died with\n   `OSError: [Errno 30] Read-only file system: .../date=2026-09-15.parquet`. Two problems worth\n   fixing: the `+26h` buffer should be `time_length + tolerance`, not a constant sized for the\n   24-hour competition; and the ingest should be gated by the offline flag, because in a campaign\n   with a data cutoff an implicit fetch for post-cutoff prices is exactly the thing the rules\n   forbid. It only failed here because the mount happened to be read-only.\n\n## On the scoring rule","originalFile":"# Suggestions\n\nNotes for whoever runs the next campaign, or maintains the harness.\n\n## On the harness and the data\n\n1. **`SYNTH_BACKTESTER_OFFLINE_DATA_ROOT` is documented in CAMPAIGN.md but not set in the\n   sandbox**, and there is no offline `miner_scores` bundle. `backtest()` therefore reaches the\n   live Synth API for the field and the rewards history on every call (~20 s of the ~60 s a\n   `predict.py` run takes, and 700 k rows for one `--days 7` run). Shipping the bundle, or\n   setting the variable to `market_data/`, would make the official path several times faster and\n   fully offline.\n\n2. **`_fill_gaps_from_realized_paths` silently falls back to one API request per prompt** when it\n   does not think it is offline. A local grid of start times never matches the validator's\n   irregular prompt times, so the prefetch considers every prompt uncached, issues requests\n   0.6 s apart, and then cannot write the cache because `market_data/` is mounted read-only. The\n   symptom is a sweep that hangs at 0 % CPU with no error. Setting the offline root around the\n   call restricts it to `RealizedPathStore.get`, which snaps within 30 minutes — the same\n   tolerance `backtest()` uses. Worth either a warning or a `max_requests` guard.\n\n3. **`_slice_real_prices` takes `.iloc[:, 0]`.** Passing a full OHLCV frame scores against\n   `open` instead of `close`, and the resulting CRPS shift (−2.5 % here) is small enough to look\n   like a modelling result rather than a bug. A column check would be cheap.\n\n4. **Competition lookup must be keyed on `(asset, time_length)`.** BTC/ETH/SOL/XRP/HYPE belong to\n   both `CRYPTO_24H` and `CRYPTO_1H`; the obvious `{a: c for c in comps for a in c.asset_list}`\n   comprehension silently scores 24h prompts with the 1h interval set (+80 % CRPS). Perhaps\n   `competition_config` could expose a helper.\n\n5. **The parquet index is `datetime64[us]`, not `[ns]`.** `index.view(\"int64\") // 60e9` — the\n   idiom most people reach for — yields a minute index that barely advances, which made every\n   return look like it spanned a gap and silently emptied my volatility sample. Models looked\n   identical and 32 % worse than baseline with no error anywhere. `np.asarray(idx.values,\n   dtype=\"datetime64[m]\").astype(\"int64\")` is unit-independent.\n\n6. **\"No trade\" and \"no move\" are indistinguishable in this data**, and roughly half of a thin\n   asset's overnight minutes are one or the other. Any robust statistic has to be chosen with\n   that in mind — a bucket median |return| collapses to exactly 0 and produced the single worst\n   regression of this campaign (+10 % on XAU).\n\n## On `agent/predict.py`\n\nA1. **`generate()` cannot produce predictions for most of the asset universe.** It builds the\n   context with `MinutePriceStore.load_range`, which raises on a single NaN close. But the model\n   contract states that `context_prices` MAY CONTAIN NaN and that the model must drop rather than\n   interpolate — exactly the situation every com-equ asset (and HYPE) is in. A `--days 7` run\n   aborts with `Local store has 3 missing minute prices in the requested range`, so out of the\n   box the official evaluation only works on BTC/ETH/SOL/XRP. I had to swap that one line for a\n   lenient partition read (reindexed onto a gapless minute grid) to score 14 of the 18\n   asset/configuration combinations. The scoring call is untouched. Worth fixing upstream, since\n   the strict loader is right for realized paths and wrong for contexts.\n\nA2. **`PREDICTIONS_DIR` is a fixed shared path that is never cleared**, and the prediction\n   filenames depend only on `(start, asset, time_length)`. Two `predict.py` runs of *different\n   models* therefore silently overwrite each other's files, and `backtest()` scores whatever is\n   on disk. I lost a 25-minute evaluation to this — a champion and a baseline running\n   concurrently produced plausible-looking but meaningless numbers, with no warning. Either\n   derive the directory from the model identity, or wipe it at the start of a run.\n\n## On the scoring rule\n\n7. **The score is a sum of univariate CRPS terms on interval returns.** Nothing in it is\n   multivariate, so the joint law of a simulated path is irrelevant except through the marginals\n   of aggregated returns. Cross-asset correlation, realistic microstructure, jump clustering and\n   momentum are all unpriced. If the subnet wants path realism it has to price it — a rank\n   correlation or a multivariate energy score term would do it. As it stands, 1000 paths is a\n   very expensive way to transmit 289 marginal distributions.\n\n8. **`E CRPS(σ)/E CRPS(σ*) ≈ 1 + 0.25·(log σ − log σ*)²`.** This is worth stating in the miner\n   documentation. It tells a new miner immediately that global calibration is nearly free\n   (±20 % costs 1 %) and that the entire competitive surface is the *conditional* variance of the\n   log volatility forecast. I would have saved two hours by knowing it at the start.\n\n9. **The prompt anchor is stale by design.** Context ends at `start − (ISSUANCE_LEAD + 60)` s\n   while `real_prices[0]` is the close of the bar labelled `start` — 180 s of unobserved drift for\n   24h prompts, 120 s for 1h. The relative-change intervals cancel it; only `24hour_abs` (3.6 %\n   of the budget) sees it, and only as ~0.1 % extra dispersion. Correctly not worth modelling,\n   but it is not obvious from the code and took a while to convince myself of.\n\n## Things I would try next\n\n10. **Cross-asset volatility pooling.** BTC/ETH/SOL/XRP/HYPE share a common vol factor; the thin\n    equities share the US cash session. Estimating a common factor plus an idiosyncratic loading\n    should beat five independent EWMAs, especially for the assets whose own history is thin. This\n    is the most promising untried idea — it attacks the residual directly.\n\n11. **A proper GARCH/HAR likelihood fit per asset**, rather than EWMA half-lives chosen by sweep.\n    My regression attempt (Iter 6) failed out of sample, but it was fitted on a squared-log-error\n    objective; fitting the *CRPS* objective directly, with the asymmetry in §8 respected, is a\n    different and better-posed problem.\n\n12. **Per-step calibration of the com-equ multiplier.** The eight fitted multipliers are constants,\n    but the thing they are correcting — the ratio between the RMS the estimator sees and the\n    width CRPS wants — is almost certainly a function of how dead the market is at that step.\n    A multiplier that is a function of the seasonal profile value would generalise better than\n    one number per asset.\n\n13. **Tick/volume features.** `trade_count` and `volume` are in the context and I never used\n    them. For thin assets, trade count is the most direct measure of whether the next five\n    minutes can move at all, and it is exactly the regime my constant multiplier is papering over.\n\n## Process\n\n14. I never encountered any pre-existing miner model in the workspace, so there was nothing to\n    leave unread.\n\n15. **Two disjoint validation windows was the single most valuable methodological choice.** Four\n    separate things looked like wins on the first window and were noise or worse on the second:\n    the fitted vol regression, the crypto half of the scale map, the crypto-1h half-life/pool/\n    innovation tuning, and (at the margin) `seas_gamma=1.0`. A single validation window would\n    have shipped all four.\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":58,"oldLines":6,"newStart":58,"newLines":19,"lines":["    concurrently produced plausible-looking but meaningless numbers, with no warning. Either","    derive the directory from the model identity, or wipe it at the start of a run."," ","+A3. **The backtester reaches 26 hours past the last prompt, and a missing day partition triggers a","+   network ingest into a read-only mount.** `backtest()` step 5 asks `download_price_data` for","+   `[min(start_time) − 2h, max(start_time) + 26h]`; that helper walks the range one *day* at a","+   time and sends any absent partition to `MinutePriceStore.ingest_range`. With a snapshot that","+   ends 2026-09-14 this is invisible for 24-hour prompts (the latest scorable one starts 09-12,","+   so +26 h stays inside) and fatal for 1-hour prompts (the latest starts 09-13 22:00, so +26 h","+   lands on 09-15): all five crypto-1h assets died with","+   `OSError: [Errno 30] Read-only file system: .../date=2026-09-15.parquet`. Two problems worth","+   fixing: the `+26h` buffer should be `time_length + tolerance`, not a constant sized for the","+   24-hour competition; and the ingest should be gated by the offline flag, because in a campaign","+   with a data cutoff an implicit fetch for post-cutoff prices is exactly the thing the rules","+   forbid. It only failed here because the mount happened to be read-only.","+"," ## On the scoring rule"," "," 7. **The score is a sum of univariate CRPS terms on interval returns.** Nothing in it is"]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CfYMPEmAp2Sw4WFWp6i4X","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01QCfcAP4x3WXYHL9MoiPJx2","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"/workspace/agent/report.md","old_string":"uv run agent/predict.py --asset BTC --days 7 --eval-end 2026-09-13 --num-simulations 1000\n```","new_string":"uv run agent/predict.py --asset BTC --days 7 --eval-end 2026-09-13 --num-simulations 1000\nbash agent/official_eval.sh    agent /tmp/off.json /tmp/off.log   # the 13 twenty-four-hour runs\nbash agent/official_eval_1h.sh agent /tmp/off.json /tmp/off.log   # the 5 one-hour runs\nuv run python agent/results_table.py                              # renders the tables in §6\n```"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":722,"cache_read_input_tokens":75783,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":722,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e0fdc998-0d4c-4bec-aa01-713aae481473","uuid":"19e8a54e-ba2b-48ee-893f-57a92931a781","timestamp":"2026-09-29T20:21:12.934Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QCfcAP4x3WXYHL9MoiPJx2","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /workspace/agent/report.md has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e0fdc998-0d4c-4bec-aa01-713aae481473","uuid":"642ca579-6392-4547-8507-95d15e3166b9","timestamp":"2026-09-29T20:21:12.951Z","tool_use_result":{"filePath":"/workspace/agent/report.md","oldString":"uv run agent/predict.py --asset BTC --days 7 --eval-end 2026-09-13 --num-simulations 1000\n```","newString":"uv run agent/predict.py --asset BTC --days 7 --eval-end 2026-09-13 --num-simulations 1000\nbash agent/official_eval.sh    agent /tmp/off.json /tmp/off.log   # the 13 twenty-four-hour runs\nbash agent/official_eval_1h.sh agent /tmp/off.json /tmp/off.log   # the 5 one-hour runs\nuv run python agent/results_table.py                              # renders the tables in §6\n```","originalFile":"# Forecasting price distributions for Synth SN50: what moved the CRPS and what didn't\n\n*A campaign report for Synth miners. Everything here was derived from the public snapshot, the\nsynth-lib backtester and the Synth API; no proprietary model informed it.*\n\n---\n\n## 1. The problem, stated precisely\n\nA Synth validator hands you an asset, a start time, a horizon and a step size, and asks for 1000\nsimulated price paths. It scores you with CRPS — lower is better. Three configurations are live:\n\n| configuration | assets | horizon / step | points |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| CRYPTO_24H | BTC ETH SOL XRP HYPE | 86400 s / 300 s | 289 |\n| COM_EQU_24H | XAU NVDAX GOOGLX TSLAX AAPLX WTIOIL SPCX SP500 | 86400 s / 300 s | 289 |\n| CRYPTO_1H | BTC ETH SOL XRP HYPE | 3600 s / 60 s | 61 |\n\nThe first thing worth knowing is what the score actually is, because it is not \"how realistic are\nyour paths\".\n\n**The validator score is a sum of *univariate* CRPS terms on relative price changes over\nintervals.** For a 24-hour prompt:\n\n| interval | number of terms | share of the score |\n|---|---|---|\n| 5 min | 288 | ≈ 61 % |\n| 30 min | 48 | ≈ 25 % |\n| 3 hour | 8 | ≈ 10 % |\n| 24 hour (absolute) | 1 | ≈ 3.6 % |\n\nThere is no multivariate term anywhere. The joint distribution of a path matters *only* through\nthe marginals of aggregated returns. Roughly 86 % of a 24-hour score is the marginal distribution\nof the 5-minute return at each of 288 future steps.\n\nThis has a blunt consequence: cross-asset correlation, jump clustering, order-flow realism,\nmomentum, and every other thing that makes a simulated path look like a real one are **unpriced**.\nIf you are spending effort there, you are spending it on something the scoring function cannot\nsee.\n\nCRYPTO_1H is similar in spirit: 1/2/5/15/30/60-minute intervals, plus twelve `*_gaps` terms\n(cumulative return from the start to each of minutes 5, 10, …, 60) worth ≈29 %, plus a small\nvolatility-CRPS block worth ~1 %.\n\n## 2. The identity that decides everything\n\nBecause the score is a sum of scale-family CRPS terms, you can write down what a mis-calibrated\nwidth costs. For a Gaussian forecast of scale σ against a truth of scale 1:\n\n```\nE CRPS(σ) = sqrt(2/π)·sqrt(1+σ²) − σ/sqrt(π)\n```\n\nminimised at σ = 1 with value 1/sqrt(π). Expanding around the optimum:\n\n```\nE CRPS(σ) / E CRPS(1)  ≈  1 + 0.25 · (log σ)²\n```\n\nI measured this empirically before deriving it, and the two agree to two digits: a **±20 % error\nin overall scale costs about 1 % of CRPS**, and a 5 % error costs 0.06 %.\n\nRead that the other way round and it is the whole campaign in one line:\n\n> A *constant* scale error is nearly free. The entire recoverable CRPS is\n> **Var(log σ̂ − log σ_true)** — the variance of your volatility forecast error in logs.\n\nIt even predicts the size of the prize. For BTC, sd(log realized 24-hour vol) ≈ 0.48, so a model\nwith a constant width gives up 0.25 × 0.48² ≈ 5.8 %. An oracle handed the realized volatility of\nthe very window it is predicting scored ≈6 % better than my best causal model on crypto-24h.\nThe arithmetic and the experiment agree.\n\nOne asymmetry matters in practice: the quadratic is symmetric but the exact loss is not. Too\n*wide* costs linearly and without bound; too *narrow* saturates at +41 %. So the CRPS-optimal\nforecast sits slightly *below* `exp(E[log σ])`, which is why every multiplier I fitted came out\nunder 1.\n\n## 3. The model\n\n```\nr_k = scale(asset) · v_k · s(t_k) · z ,        price_k = last · exp(cumsum(r))\n```\n\n* **`s(t_k)` — time-of-week seasonality.** A 336-bucket (half-hourly) profile per asset and step\n  size, fitted offline and committed as an artifact. The venues never close but the underlying\n  markets do: mean |5-minute return| swings ≈1.8× across the week for crypto and up to ≈6× for\n  the tokenised equities. The profile is raised to the power 0.85 — damping it beat both the raw\n  profile and no profile at all.\n* **`v_k` — conditional volatility of the *deseasonalised* returns.** A geometric blend of EWMA\n  variances at half-lives of 1 h / 6 h / 48 h, reverting log-linearly toward the 7-day level with\n  a half-life of 96 steps.\n* **`z` — standardised Student-t(4).** Gaussian innovations cost about 1 %.\n* **`scale(asset)`** — 0.90 for every crypto asset; one fitted multiplier each for the eight\n  com-equ assets (0.72–0.88).\n\nOne configuration serves all three scoring configurations. It runs offline in ~40 ms per prompt\nat 1000 paths, against a practical ceiling of about 5 s.\n\nEverything in the model is there because it survived a check on a validation window it was not\ntuned on. Most of what I tried did not.\n\n## 4. What worked\n\n### Time-of-week seasonality (−1.9 %) — the largest single win\n\nVolatility in these markets is strongly deterministic in time-of-week, and a constant σ is\nsimultaneously too wide overnight and too narrow at the US cash open. CRPS charges for both.\nDamped at γ = 0.85 this is worth −1.9 % across 13 assets, concentrated in com-equ (NVDAX −3.5 %,\nTSLAX −3.8 %, GOOGLX −3.2 %).\n\n### Student-t(4) innovations (−1 %)\n\nEssentially the entire prize available from distribution *shape*. Swapping t(4) for a normal at\nidentical scale costs ~1 %; `nu=3` and `nu=6` are both worse; an empirical bootstrap of the\ncontext returns is a wash.\n\n### Volatility mean reversion (−0.6 %)\n\nReverting the current EWMA level toward the 7-day level with a 96-step half-life. This was the\n*only* one of the vol-dynamics knobs that mattered — see §5.\n\n### A per-asset width multiplier for com-equ (−0.13 % out of sample)\n\nFitted on a window preceding both validation windows. Small, but it is the part of the per-asset\ncalibration that demonstrably generalises, and the diagnosis is the interesting bit (§5).\n\n## 5. What didn't work — the more useful half\n\n### Median-based seasonality: +10 % on XAU\n\nMy first seasonality profile used the bucket **median** |return| and made things dramatically\nworse. In a thin market's quiet hours most 5-minute returns are *exactly zero* — no trade — so\nthe median collapses toward 0 while the width CRPS actually wants does not. The model became\nabsurdly narrow overnight and was punished for it.\n\nSwitching to a **winsorised RMS** (clipped at 6 robust sigmas; XRP's raw return kurtosis on this\nsnapshot is about 7000, so a plain RMS is set by single prints) turned a +0.35 % regression into\na −1.9 % improvement.\n\n> When a feature makes things worse, the feature is usually right and the *estimator* is wrong.\n> And in this data, \"no trade\" and \"no move\" are the same observation.\n\n### A fitted volatility regression: better forecast, worse score\n\n§2 says the prize is Var(log σ̂ − log σ). That is a regression target, so I regressed it: log\nfuture volatility on the log EWMA vols of the deseasonalised context at five half-lives, per\nasset, at five horizon knots, so the fitted cumulative-variance curve could be differentiated\ninto a per-step term structure.\n\nIn-sample R² was 0.36–0.73. Measured against realized volatility it *was* a better forecast than\nthe EWMA blend on crypto (log-error sd 0.281 vs 0.314 for BTC). It still lost on CRPS, and it\ntook three separate fixes to even find out why:\n\n1. **`np.interp` clamps below its first knot.** The cumulative variance curve came out flat over\n   the first steps — literally zero dispersion at steps 1 and 2. CRPS punishes a zero-width\n   forecast savagely.\n2. **The fitted \"term structure\" was almost entirely small-sample log bias.** The target is\n   `log(RMS of h returns)` and log is concave, so `E[log RMS₃]` sits ~45 % below `log σ` for\n   fat-tailed returns while `E[log RMS₂₈₈]` is within 0.3 %. Fitting each horizon separately\n   manufactures a steep upward slope that is pure estimator artefact. The fix is to measure the\n   bias on non-overlapping blocks of the same series and subtract it.\n3. **The A/B test was not testing what I thought.** With a 96-step reversion half-life over a\n   288-step path, most of the path has already reverted to the long-run level, so \"swapping in\n   the regression\" barely used the regression.\n\nAfter all three fixes, at each model's individually optimal multiplier the fitted and EWMA levels\n**tie** (mean excess loss 3.40 % vs 3.37 %). Out of sample on the later window, the regression was\nworse on exactly the assets it was supposed to help: HYPE +1.12 %, XRP +0.74 %, SOL +0.53 %.\n\nRejected. The code stays in the repo as a *measurement instrument* — its residual sd is what tells\nme 3.6 % (24h) and 2.5 % (1h) of CRPS remain on the table.\n\n### Almost every volatility-dynamics knob\n\nSwept `revert_halflife` ∈ {24,48,96,192,384}, three half-life triples, three weight vectors,\nvol-of-vol ∈ {0.2,0.35,0.5}, winsorisation ∈ {2.5,3.5,6}, and an L1 variance estimator. Only\n`revert_halflife` moved the mean by more than 0.1 %. This is exactly what §2 predicts: these knobs\nmove the *level*, and the level is nearly free.\n\n### Per-asset scales for crypto\n\nThe same calibration that produced good com-equ multipliers produced crypto multipliers that were\nnoise. Mapping all 13 assets gave −0.21 % on the first window and **+0.13 % on the second**;\ncom-equ only gave −0.21 % and −0.13 %.\n\nWhat separated them was *stability under refitting*. Refitted four months apart on disjoint data,\nthe com-equ multipliers barely moved — GOOGLX 0.746 → 0.741, NVDAX 0.761 → 0.755, SPCX 0.831 →\n0.835, XAU 0.764 → 0.773 — while the crypto ones wandered (HYPE 1.074 → 0.984). Parameter\nstability under refitting predicted out-of-sample transfer better than in-sample gain did.\n\n### A bespoke crypto-1h model\n\nA 60-minute horizon at 1-minute resolution *ought* to want faster volatility dynamics. It does\nnot: the (2,10,60)-minute blend is 0.41 % worse than (12,72,576). Slower half-lives, a 7-day pool\nand empirical innovations together looked worth −0.43 % on the first window and delivered nothing\non the second (all variants within ±0.08 %, the unchanged configuration marginally ahead). So\ncrypto-1h ships with the 24-hour settings untouched.\n\n### Return term-structure correction\n\nEmpirical variance ratios are below 1 (BTC 0.92 at 5-hour horizons, AAPLX 0.67), so an i.i.d.\nmodel over-disperses the 30-minute and 3-hour blocks. Matching them needs an MA(1) coefficient of\nabout −0.04, which corrects a ~4 % scale error on 39 % of the budget. By §2 that is worth\n0.25 × 0.04² ≈ 0.04 % of those terms. Computed, not implemented — the identity in §2 is very good\nat telling you what *not* to build.\n\n## 6. Results\n\nBacktested with `agent/predict.py` (the official `synth_lib.backtester`), 7 days of prompts,\n1000 simulated paths — the same count the validator serves. \"Starting point\" is the log-normal\nmartingale this repo ships with, run through exactly the same path. The 24-hour legs end\n2026-09-13; the 1-hour leg ends 2026-09-12, because the backtester pulls minute prices out to\n`max(start_time) + 26 h` and the 1-hour prompt set otherwise crosses the edge of the snapshot\n(journal, Iter 10). Both models are scored on identical prompt sets.\n\n**CRYPTO_24H**\n\n| asset | prompts | champion CRPS | starting point CRPS | change |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| BTC | 159 | 2132.8 | 2274.3 | -6.22% |\n| ETH | 155 | 2990.1 | 3144.3 | -4.90% |\n| SOL | 168 | 3743.6 | 3873.4 | -3.35% |\n| XRP | 169 | 3878.0 | 4070.9 | -4.74% |\n| HYPE | 162 | 4956.1 | 5037.5 | -1.62% |\n| **mean** | | **3540.1** | **3680.1** | **-3.80%** |\n\n**COM_EQU_24H**\n\n| asset | prompts | champion CRPS | starting point CRPS | change |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| XAU | 169 | 1373.4 | 1556.7 | -11.78% |\n| NVDAX | 167 | 1612.5 | 1790.4 | -9.93% |\n| GOOGLX | 167 | 1501.7 | 1642.3 | -8.56% |\n| TSLAX | 157 | 1993.0 | 2416.1 | -17.51% |\n| AAPLX | 168 | 1607.8 | 1886.3 | -14.77% |\n| WTIOIL | 167 | 3198.7 | 3376.6 | -5.27% |\n| SPCX | 168 | 2818.5 | 3269.6 | -13.79% |\n| SP500 | 161 | 638.2 | 673.7 | -5.26% |\n| **mean** | | **1843.0** | **2076.5** | **-11.24%** |\n\n**CRYPTO_1H**\n\n| asset | prompts | champion CRPS | starting point CRPS | change |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| BTC | 990 | 472.9 | 508.8 | -7.05% |\n| ETH | 956 | 667.8 | 703.7 | -5.10% |\n| SOL | 977 | 880.2 | 904.9 | -2.74% |\n| XRP | 979 | 893.2 | 937.7 | -4.74% |\n| HYPE | 992 | 1083.1 | 1104.5 | -1.94% |\n| **mean** | | **799.5** | **831.9** | **-3.90%** |\n\nThe champion is ahead on **all 18** asset/configuration combinations. The spread across\nconfigurations is itself informative: com-equ gains three times what crypto gains, because the\ntokenised equities are where the deterministic time-of-week structure lives. A constant-width\nmodel is grossly miscalibrated across a market that is open six and a half hours a day, and §2\nsays that miscalibration is priced as a *variance* of log width, not a level.\n\nThe gain is smallest on HYPE (−1.6 % / −1.9 %), the asset with the thinnest history and the\nnoisiest volatility profile — consistent with the picture in §5 that what generalises is the part\nof the estimate that is stable under refitting.\n\nFor scale: over 2026-07-10→12, the *best miner in the live field* on BTC scored 1841.4, with a\nmedian of 1997 and p75 of 2016 across ~200 miners — many of them identical to within 0.1, which\nsuggests a widely shared template. The starting-point model in this repo is not competitive with\nthat; the champion is well inside it.\n\n## 7. Limitations\n\n* **The evaluation window is 10 days of live trading that I cannot see.** Every number here is a\n  backtest. The com-equ multipliers are stable across four months of history, but a volatility\n  regime change in the evaluation window would hurt com-equ more than crypto.\n* **The seasonality artifact is fitted on the whole snapshot**, so the backtested numbers in §6\n  are mildly optimistic relative to a truly causal run. The validation numbers quoted throughout\n  §4–§5 all use artifacts fitted strictly before their window, and the effects survive there.\n* **Two tokenised equities (SPCX, SP500) have short histories** — SP500 only from the mid-July\n  SPYX retirement — so their profiles and multipliers rest on less data than the others.\n* **The model has no drift and no cross-asset structure.** Both are defensible given §1, but the\n  second is the most promising thing I left on the table (§8).\n* **`num_simulations` is honoured as passed**, but everything was tuned at 1000. Empirical CRPS is\n  biased upward at small sample sizes; at 100 paths the absolute numbers would be worse, though\n  the ranking between variants was stable in spot checks.\n\n## 8. What I would do with more budget\n\n1. **Cross-asset volatility pooling.** BTC/ETH/SOL/XRP/HYPE share a common volatility factor and\n   the tokenised equities share the US cash session. A common factor plus per-asset loadings\n   should beat 13 independent EWMAs — most of all for the assets whose own history is thin. This\n   attacks the residual variance directly, which §2 says is the only thing that pays.\n2. **Fit the CRPS objective, not a squared-log-error proxy.** My regression minimised the wrong\n   loss and respected neither the asymmetry in §2 nor the seasonal weighting. The CRPS-optimal\n   forecast is a different, better-posed estimation problem.\n3. **Use `volume` and `trade_count`.** They sit unused in the context. For a thin asset, trade\n   count is the most direct measure of whether the next five minutes *can* move — which is\n   precisely the regime my constant per-asset multiplier is papering over.\n4. **Make the com-equ multiplier a function of the seasonal level** rather than a constant. The\n   thing it corrects (the gap between the RMS the estimator sees and the width CRPS wants) is\n   almost certainly a function of how dead the market is at that step.\n\n## 9. Reproducing this\n\n```bash\nuv run python agent/seasonality.py                  # rebuild the time-of-week artifacts\nuv run python agent/calibrate.py dev                # refit the per-asset multipliers\nuv run agent/predict.py --asset BTC --days 7 --eval-end 2026-09-13 --num-simulations 1000\n```\n\n`agent/harness.py` + `agent/evaluate.py` + `agent/sweep.py` are the fast iteration loop — 545\nprompts × 1000 paths × 5 assets in 16 s, against ~20 s per asset-day for the official path. It\ncomputes CRPS with the very same `calculate_total_score_for_miner` the validator calls, on\nrealized paths assembled the way `backtest()` assembles them, and it is cross-checked against\n`predict.py` in `journal.md` (2740.9 vs 2743.4 on an identical prediction file). Every number\nquoted as a *result* still goes through `predict.py`.\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":302,"oldLines":6,"newStart":302,"newLines":9,"lines":[" uv run python agent/seasonality.py                  # rebuild the time-of-week artifacts"," uv run python agent/calibrate.py dev                # refit the per-asset multipliers"," uv run agent/predict.py --asset BTC --days 7 --eval-end 2026-09-13 --num-simulations 1000","+bash agent/official_eval.sh    agent /tmp/off.json /tmp/off.log   # the 13 twenty-four-hour runs","+bash agent/official_eval_1h.sh agent /tmp/off.json /tmp/off.log   # the 5 one-hour runs","+uv run python agent/results_table.py                              # renders the tables in §6"," ```"," "," `agent/harness.py` + `agent/evaluate.py` + `agent/sweep.py` are the fast iteration loop — 545"]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false}}
