{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LTuGN91SrY5cxqYvX4qyDT","type":"tool_result","content":"### Almost every volatility-dynamics knob\n\nSwept `revert_halflife` ∈ {24,48,96,192,384}, three half-life triples, three weight vectors,\nvol-of-vol ∈ {0.2,0.35,0.5}, winsorisation ∈ {2.5,3.5,6}, and an L1 variance estimator. Only\n`revert_halflife` moved the mean by more than 0.1 %. This is exactly what §2 predicts: these knobs\nmove the *level*, and the level is nearly free.\n\n### Per-asset scales for crypto\n\nThe same calibration that produced good com-equ multipliers produced crypto multipliers that were\nnoise. Mapping all 13 assets gave −0.21 % on the first window and **+0.13 % on the second**;\ncom-equ only gave −0.21 % and −0.13 %.\n\nWhat separated them was *stability under refitting*. Refitted four months apart on disjoint data,\nthe com-equ multipliers barely moved — GOOGLX 0.746 → 0.741, NVDAX 0.761 → 0.755, SPCX 0.831 →\n0.835, XAU 0.764 → 0.773 — while the crypto ones wandered (HYPE 1.074 → 0.984). Parameter\nstability under refitting predicted out-of-sample transfer better than in-sample gain did.\n\n### A bespoke crypto-1h model\n\nA 60-minute horizon at 1-minute resolution *ought* to want faster volatility dynamics. It does\nnot: the (2,10,60)-minute blend is 0.41 % worse than (12,72,576). Slower half-lives, a 7-day pool\nand empirical innovations together looked worth −0.43 % on the first window and delivered nothing\non the second (all variants within ±0.08 %, the unchanged configuration marginally ahead). So\ncrypto-1h ships with the 24-hour settings untouched.\n\n### Return term-structure correction","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e0fdc998-0d4c-4bec-aa01-713aae481473","uuid":"c779eb3e-f9e2-4815-a8f2-d14d0e2a2641","timestamp":"2026-09-29T22:52:30.560Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"### Almost every volatility-dynamics knob\n\nSwept `revert_halflife` ∈ {24,48,96,192,384}, three half-life triples, three weight vectors,\nvol-of-vol ∈ {0.2,0.35,0.5}, winsorisation ∈ {2.5,3.5,6}, and an L1 variance estimator. Only\n`revert_halflife` moved the mean by more than 0.1 %. This is exactly what §2 predicts: these knobs\nmove the *level*, and the level is nearly free.\n\n### Per-asset scales for crypto\n\nThe same calibration that produced good com-equ multipliers produced crypto multipliers that were\nnoise. Mapping all 13 assets gave −0.21 % on the first window and **+0.13 % on the second**;\ncom-equ only gave −0.21 % and −0.13 %.\n\nWhat separated them was *stability under refitting*. Refitted four months apart on disjoint data,\nthe com-equ multipliers barely moved — GOOGLX 0.746 → 0.741, NVDAX 0.761 → 0.755, SPCX 0.831 →\n0.835, XAU 0.764 → 0.773 — while the crypto ones wandered (HYPE 1.074 → 0.984). Parameter\nstability under refitting predicted out-of-sample transfer better than in-sample gain did.\n\n### A bespoke crypto-1h model\n\nA 60-minute horizon at 1-minute resolution *ought* to want faster volatility dynamics. It does\nnot: the (2,10,60)-minute blend is 0.41 % worse than (12,72,576). Slower half-lives, a 7-day pool\nand empirical innovations together looked worth −0.43 % on the first window and delivered nothing\non the second (all variants within ±0.08 %, the unchanged configuration marginally ahead). So\ncrypto-1h ships with the 24-hour settings untouched.\n\n### Return term-structure correction","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CfYYvjQz5nr5RFgxnRhwx","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01VXw646rBASTitUR3PTMBQW","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"uv run python - <<'PY'\nimport pathlib\np = pathlib.Path(\"agent/report.md\"); s = p.read_text()\nold = \"\"\"crypto-1h ships with the 24-hour settings untouched.\"\"\"\nnew = \"\"\"crypto-1h ships with the 24-hour settings untouched.\n\nIts *width level* was the last thing I checked there, since `scale = 0.90` was fitted on 5-minute\n24-hour prompts and a 1-minute return is a different random variable. It transfers: the 1-minute\noptimum is 0.95, worth −0.09 %/−0.05 %, and the per-asset optima are all within 0.2 % of the flat\nvalue. Left at 0.90.\"\"\"\nassert old in s\ns = s.replace(old, new, 1)\np.write_text(s)\nPY\ncd /workspace && git add -A agent && git commit -q -m \"$(cat <<'EOF'\nSeasonal profile resolution: 30-minute buckets are already optimal\n\nMade bucket width a parameter and had models.py read the resolution off the\nprofile's length, then swept resolution x gamma x scale jointly. Finer is\nworse (15min +0.9%, 10min +1.2% on com-equ), coarser is worse, and the best\ncandidate (30min/s3, -0.05%) is below the threshold anything has transferred\nat. Champion unchanged.\n\nThe grid separates sample-per-bucket from smoothing span: 15min/s9 spans\n135 min vs the shipped 150 and still loses 0.9%, so it is the sample size.\n\nThis also falsifies the reason I had written down for the 1-minute damping --\nrefining the grid 6x leaves the 1-minute step unchanged to three decimals.\nCorrected in modeling.py and report.md; the parameter itself is unaffected.\n\nAlso: fit_profile returned an all-NaN profile when no bucket cleared the\n20-observation threshold (SPCX at 10-minute buckets), which would have served\nNaN prices rather than failing. It now falls back to flat.\n\nAlso checked and left alone: the global width level at the 1-minute step.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>\nEOF\n)\" && git log --oneline -1 && cat agent/BUDGET.md","description":"Commit resolution experiment and writeups"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":815,"cache_read_input_tokens":91228,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":815,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e0fdc998-0d4c-4bec-aa01-713aae481473","uuid":"99f09e1a-1c67-4fa2-a8f4-d0ea70647660","timestamp":"2026-09-29T22:52:39.712Z"}
