{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CfYcVZUxaxj6FseVxA4Po","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01YVjuvLsr9GL93aBsRFv4Ri","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"uv run python - <<'PY'\nimport pathlib\np = pathlib.Path(\"agent/report.md\"); s = p.read_text()\nold = \"\"\"Swept `revert_halflife` ∈ {24,48,96,192,384}, three half-life triples, three weight vectors,\nvol-of-vol ∈ {0.2,0.35,0.5}, winsorisation ∈ {2.5,3.5,6}, and an L1 variance estimator. Only\n`revert_halflife` moved the mean by more than 0.1 %. This is exactly what §2 predicts: these knobs\nmove the *level*, and the level is nearly free.\"\"\"\nnew = \"\"\"Swept `revert_halflife` ∈ {24,48,96,192,384}, three half-life triples, three weight vectors,\nvol-of-vol ∈ {0.2,0.35,0.5}, winsorisation ∈ {2.5,3.5,6}, and an L1 variance estimator. Only\n`revert_halflife` moved the mean by more than 0.1 %. This is exactly what §2 predicts: these knobs\nmove the *level*, and the level is nearly free.\n\nThe vol-of-vol entry in that list deserves a footnote, because the same idea came back later and\nworked. What was swept there was a *constant-per-path* log-level shock, which fattens the\naggregated intervals and the per-step marginal alike — and the per-step marginal, 61 % of the\nscore, was already calibrated, so it lost more than it gained. Constraining the factor to leave\nevery step's variance exactly unchanged is what turned it into the −0.10 % in §4. The idea was\nnever wrong; the unconstrained version of it was.\"\"\"\nassert s.count(old) == 1\ns = s.replace(old, new, 1)\n\nold3 = \"\"\"5. **Re-audit every other parameter the way γ was re-audited.**\"\"\"\nnew3 = \"\"\"5. **Push on the score's shape, not its level.** §4's last entry is a single AR(1) factor with\n   one fitted number, and it is the only thing that has ever paid on the aggregated intervals.\n   The obvious extensions: a term structure for it (the 3-hour and 24-hour terms want more\n   clustering than the 30-minute one), and fitting it per asset rather than per group. I stopped\n   because the campaign ran out of budget, not because the seam was exhausted.\n6. **Re-audit every other parameter the way γ was re-audited.**\"\"\"\nassert s.count(old3) == 1\ns = s.replace(old3, new3, 1)\np.write_text(s)\nprint(\"ok\")\nPY\ngrep -n \"^[0-9]\\. \\*\\*\" agent/report.md | tail -8","description":"Update report section 5 and 8"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1252,"cache_read_input_tokens":139921,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1252,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e0fdc998-0d4c-4bec-aa01-713aae481473","uuid":"ba26d642-0182-476e-9a7e-f8fa4a3b6752","timestamp":"2026-09-29T23:39:29.477Z"}
