{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CfYfMQ3ZpfQtjdWChLFbr","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":412,"cache_read_input_tokens":58729,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":412,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e0fdc998-0d4c-4bec-aa01-713aae481473","uuid":"517f0ec8-413c-4bc1-961c-42a06684a511","timestamp":"2026-09-30T00:16:49.225Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CfYfMQ3ZpfQtjdWChLFbr","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_019L54jdpH4L8282fHC8GGUT","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"uv run python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np = Path(\"agent/report.md\"); s = p.read_text()\n\n# 1. the crypto-1h width paragraph is stale -- the step-keyed level did ship\nold = \"\"\"Its *width level* was the last thing I checked there, since `scale = 0.90` was fitted on 5-minute\n24-hour prompts and a 1-minute return is a different random variable. It transfers: the 1-minute\noptimum is 0.95, worth −0.09 %/−0.05 %, and the per-asset optima are all within 0.2 % of the flat\nvalue. Left at 0.90.\"\"\"\nnew = \"\"\"Its *width level*, on the other hand, did not transfer, and that is the one thing about the\n1-hour configuration that ships differently. `scale = 0.90` was fitted on 5-minute returns over\n24-hour prompts, and a 1-minute return is a different random variable: about half of them are\nexactly zero on the thinner books, so the RMS the estimator reports is depressed relative to the\nwidth CRPS actually wants. The 1-minute optimum is 0.95, worth −0.085 %/−0.215 %/−0.049 % on the\nthree windows. The per-asset optima around it are all within 0.2 % of the flat value, so the level\nis keyed on the *step size* and nothing else: `scale = {5: 0.90, 1: 0.95}`. Applying the same 0.95\nto crypto-24h flips the sign, which is the tell that this is a property of the 1-minute return and\nnot a better global calibration.\"\"\"\nassert s.count(old) == 1; s = s.replace(old, new)\n\n# 2. leverage subsection + the redundancy framing, inserted before the dynamics-knob subsection\nanchor = \"### Almost every volatility-dynamics knob\"\nassert s.count(anchor) == 1\nlev = \"\"\"### The leverage effect\n\nEquity volatility rises after price falls; it is one of the most robust stylised facts there is,\nand both standard ways of writing it down are cheap to add to a model that already carries an\nEWMA. I tried both: a multiplier driven by the exponentially-weighted *downside* return only, and\none driven by the z-scored signed return, each over half-lives from 30 minutes to 2 days and\ncoefficients from small to implausible. Every setting was flat or worse on every window and both\nasset classes; the best cell of the whole grid was −0.02 %, which is a tie.\n\n### Why those three failures are the same failure\n\nRange estimators, trade count and volume, and the leverage effect are three unrelated features\nfrom three unrelated literatures, and they failed in exactly the same way: real signal in a\nregression against future volatility, and zero CRPS once they were added to the model. The reason\nis structural. All three act on future volatility *through* realized volatility — a wide bar, a\nbusy minute and a sharp drop are all ways of saying the market just moved a lot — and a 12-minute\nEWMA of realized volatility has already read that. What looks like new information is old\ninformation arriving by a shorter path.\n\nThe useful half of this is the corollary. Information that is genuinely **forward-looking** —\nknown at the prompt time but not derivable from the price history — is not redundant with the\nEWMA and is where the gains are. There is exactly one such feature in this model, the time-of-week\nprofile, and it is the largest single win of the campaign by an order of magnitude. That is not a\ncoincidence, and it is the first thing I would tell anyone starting here: before testing a\nfeature, ask whether a fast EWMA of realized volatility can already see it. If it can, test the\n*increment* over the EWMA from the start rather than the feature's standalone predictive power,\nand you will save the four hours I spent finding this out three times.\n\n\"\"\"\ns = s.replace(anchor, lev + anchor)\n\n# 3. the dynamics-knob subsection gets the post-change re-audit\nold2 = \"\"\"move the *level*, and the level is nearly free.\"\"\"\nnew2 = \"\"\"move the *level*, and the level is nearly free.\n\nRe-swept once more at the very end, after the vol-of-vol factor below had changed the shape of the\nσ path, in case the factor had displaced any of them. It had not: every alternative half-life set,\nweight vector, reversion half-life and winsorisation threshold came back inside ±0.1 % on all\nthree windows, and the only candidate with a consistent sign was consistently worse. The factor\nis orthogonal to the drift of the deterministic σ path by construction — it preserves each step's\nvariance exactly — and the re-audit is the confirmation that the construction held.\"\"\"\nassert s.count(old2) == 1; s = s.replace(old2, new2)\np.write_text(s)\nprint(\"ok\")\nPY\nsed -n '419,455p' agent/report.md","description":"Patch report sections 5"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":412,"cache_read_input_tokens":58729,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":412,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e0fdc998-0d4c-4bec-aa01-713aae481473","uuid":"150ee8bf-b5ae-4589-b7cd-9f0f6587d2b2","timestamp":"2026-09-30T00:17:08.197Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019L54jdpH4L8282fHC8GGUT","type":"tool_result","content":"ok\n| HYPE | 992 | 1083.1 | 1104.5 | -1.94% |\n| **mean** | | **799.5** | **831.9** | **-3.90%** |\n\nThe champion is ahead on **all 18** asset/configuration combinations. The spread across\nconfigurations is itself informative: com-equ gains three times what crypto gains, because the\ntokenised equities are where the deterministic time-of-week structure lives. A constant-width\nmodel is grossly miscalibrated across a market that is open six and a half hours a day, and §2\nsays that miscalibration is priced as a *variance* of log width, not a level.\n\nThe gain is smallest on HYPE (−2.0 % / −1.9 %), the asset with the thinnest history and the\nnoisiest volatility profile — consistent with the picture in §5 that what generalises is the part\nof the estimate that is stable under refitting.\n\nFor scale: over 2026-07-10→12, the *best miner in the live field* on BTC scored 1841.4, with a\nmedian of 1997 and p75 of 2016 across ~200 miners — many of them identical to within 0.1, which\nsuggests a widely shared template. The starting-point model in this repo is not competitive with\nthat; the champion is well inside it.\n\n## 7. Limitations\n\n* **The evaluation window is 10 days of live trading that I cannot see.** Every number here is a\n  backtest. The com-equ multipliers are stable across four months of history, but a volatility\n  regime change in the evaluation window would hurt com-equ more than crypto.\n* **The seasonality artifact is fitted on the whole snapshot**, so the backtested numbers in §6\n  are mildly optimistic relative to a truly causal run. The validation numbers quoted throughout\n  §4–§5 all use artifacts fitted strictly before their window, and the effects survive there.\n* **Two tokenised equities (SPCX, SP500) have short histories** — SP500 only from the mid-July\n  SPYX retirement — so their profiles and multipliers rest on less data than the others.\n* **The model has no drift and no cross-asset structure.** Both are defensible given §1, but the\n  second is the most promising thing I left on the table (§8).\n* **The last two parameters had no untouched window left.** The AR(1) dispersion (0.30) and the\n  level that goes with it were chosen by looking at all three validation windows at once. They\n  agree to within 0.006 % across those three, which is why I believe them, but \"consistent on\n  three windows I looked at\" is a weaker claim than the earlier results, where a window was held\n  back and only consulted once. Same for the 1-minute level. Both are small effects, and both are\n  the kind §2 says are cheap to get slightly wrong.\n* **`num_simulations` is honoured as passed**, but everything was tuned at 1000. Empirical CRPS is","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e0fdc998-0d4c-4bec-aa01-713aae481473","uuid":"b6f6e15e-c43f-48f3-9f2f-d4e0823b679f","timestamp":"2026-09-30T00:17:08.344Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"ok\n| HYPE | 992 | 1083.1 | 1104.5 | -1.94% |\n| **mean** | | **799.5** | **831.9** | **-3.90%** |\n\nThe champion is ahead on **all 18** asset/configuration combinations. The spread across\nconfigurations is itself informative: com-equ gains three times what crypto gains, because the\ntokenised equities are where the deterministic time-of-week structure lives. A constant-width\nmodel is grossly miscalibrated across a market that is open six and a half hours a day, and §2\nsays that miscalibration is priced as a *variance* of log width, not a level.\n\nThe gain is smallest on HYPE (−2.0 % / −1.9 %), the asset with the thinnest history and the\nnoisiest volatility profile — consistent with the picture in §5 that what generalises is the part\nof the estimate that is stable under refitting.\n\nFor scale: over 2026-07-10→12, the *best miner in the live field* on BTC scored 1841.4, with a\nmedian of 1997 and p75 of 2016 across ~200 miners — many of them identical to within 0.1, which\nsuggests a widely shared template. The starting-point model in this repo is not competitive with\nthat; the champion is well inside it.\n\n## 7. Limitations\n\n* **The evaluation window is 10 days of live trading that I cannot see.** Every number here is a\n  backtest. The com-equ multipliers are stable across four months of history, but a volatility\n  regime change in the evaluation window would hurt com-equ more than crypto.\n* **The seasonality artifact is fitted on the whole snapshot**, so the backtested numbers in §6\n  are mildly optimistic relative to a truly causal run. The validation numbers quoted throughout\n  §4–§5 all use artifacts fitted strictly before their window, and the effects survive there.\n* **Two tokenised equities (SPCX, SP500) have short histories** — SP500 only from the mid-July\n  SPYX retirement — so their profiles and multipliers rest on less data than the others.\n* **The model has no drift and no cross-asset structure.** Both are defensible given §1, but the\n  second is the most promising thing I left on the table (§8).\n* **The last two parameters had no untouched window left.** The AR(1) dispersion (0.30) and the\n  level that goes with it were chosen by looking at all three validation windows at once. They\n  agree to within 0.006 % across those three, which is why I believe them, but \"consistent on\n  three windows I looked at\" is a weaker claim than the earlier results, where a window was held\n  back and only consulted once. Same for the 1-minute level. Both are small effects, and both are\n  the kind §2 says are cheap to get slightly wrong.\n* **`num_simulations` is honoured as passed**, but everything was tuned at 1000. Empirical CRPS is","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"bashEditDiff":{"files":[{"filePath":"/workspace/agent/report.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":288,"oldLines":6,"newStart":288,"newLines":34,"lines":[" Rejected. The code stays in the repo as a *measurement instrument* — its residual sd is what tells"," me 3.6 % (24h) and 2.5 % (1h) of CRPS remain on the table."," ","+### The leverage effect","+","+Equity volatility rises after price falls; it is one of the most robust stylised facts there is,","+and both standard ways of writing it down are cheap to add to a model that already carries an","+EWMA. I tried both: a multiplier driven by the exponentially-weighted *downside* return only, and","+one driven by the z-scored signed return, each over half-lives from 30 minutes to 2 days and","+coefficients from small to implausible. Every setting was flat or worse on every window and both","+asset classes; the best cell of the whole grid was −0.02 %, which is a tie.","+","+### Why those three failures are the same failure","+","+Range estimators, trade count and volume, and the leverage effect are three unrelated features","+from three unrelated literatures, and they failed in exactly the same way: real signal in a","+regression against future volatility, and zero CRPS once they were added to the model. The reason","+is structural. All three act on future volatility *through* realized volatility — a wide bar, a","+busy minute and a sharp drop are all ways of saying the market just moved a lot — and a 12-minute","+EWMA of realized volatility has already read that. What looks like new information is old","+information arriving by a shorter path.","+","+The useful half of this is the corollary. Information that is genuinely **forward-looking** —","+known at the prompt time but not derivable from the price history — is not redundant with the","+EWMA and is where the gains are. There is exactly one such feature in this model, the time-of-week","+profile, and it is the largest single win of the campaign by an order of magnitude. That is not a","+coincidence, and it is the first thing I would tell anyone starting here: before testing a","+feature, ask whether a fast EWMA of realized volatility can already see it. If it can, test the","+*increment* over the EWMA from the start rather than the feature's standalone predictive power,","+and you will save the four hours I spent finding this out three times.","+"," ### Almost every volatility-dynamics knob"," "," Swept `revert_halflife` ∈ {24,48,96,192,384}, three half-life triples, three weight vectors,"]},{"oldStart":295,"oldLines":6,"newStart":323,"newLines":13,"lines":[" `revert_halflife` moved the mean by more than 0.1 %. This is exactly what §2 predicts: these knobs"," move the *level*, and the level is nearly free."," ","+Re-swept once more at the very end, after the vol-of-vol factor below had changed the shape of the","+σ path, in case the factor had displaced any of them. It had not: every alternative half-life set,","+weight vector, reversion half-life and winsorisation threshold came back inside ±0.1 % on all","+three windows, and the only candidate with a consistent sign was consistently worse. The factor","+is orthogonal to the drift of the deterministic σ path by construction — it preserves each step's","+variance exactly — and the re-audit is the confirmation that the construction held.","+"," The vol-of-vol entry in that list deserves a footnote, because the same idea came back later and"," worked. What was swept there was a *constant-per-path* log-level shock, which fattens the"," aggregated intervals and the per-step marginal alike — and the per-step marginal, 61 % of the"]},{"oldStart":321,"oldLines":10,"newStart":356,"newLines":15,"lines":[" on the second (all variants within ±0.08 %, the unchanged configuration marginally ahead). So"," crypto-1h ships with the 24-hour settings untouched."," ","-Its *width level* was the last thing I checked there, since `scale = 0.90` was fitted on 5-minute","-24-hour prompts and a 1-minute return is a different random variable. It transfers: the 1-minute","-optimum is 0.95, worth −0.09 %/−0.05 %, and the per-asset optima are all within 0.2 % of the flat","-value. Left at 0.90.","+Its *width level*, on the other hand, did not transfer, and that is the one thing about the","+1-hour configuration that ships differently. `scale = 0.90` was fitted on 5-minute returns over","+24-hour prompts, and a 1-minute return is a different random variable: about half of them are","+exactly zero on the thinner books, so the RMS the estimator reports is depressed relative to the","+width CRPS actually wants. The 1-minute optimum is 0.95, worth −0.085 %/−0.215 %/−0.049 % on the","+three windows. The per-asset optima around it are all within 0.2 % of the flat value, so the level","+is keyed on the *step size* and nothing else: `scale = {5: 0.90, 1: 0.95}`. Applying the same 0.95","+to crypto-24h flips the sign, which is the tell that this is a property of the 1-minute return and","+not a better global calibration."," "," ### Return term-structure correction"," "]}]}],"moreFiles":0,"changedFiles":["/workspace/agent/report.md"]}}}
