{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CfYkbJrV2aKAg3Sjdq3XV","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01TTTEaNapRkDoV6Ddg7W1KF","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cat >> agent/journal.md <<'EOF'\n\n---\n\n## Iteration 23 — Does the time-of-week profile flatten in a hot regime?\n\n**Hypothesis**: the seasonal profile is a fixed shape, scaled up and down by the EWMA. But a macro\nshock does not respect trading hours — news arrives overnight and the tokenised equities have to\nprice it at whatever hour it lands — so in a hot regime the quiet buckets should catch up and the\nday/night ratio should compress. This is the one interaction left that is not redundant with the\nEWMA under the Iter 16 principle: neither the calendar alone nor the realized volatility alone\ncontains it, only the product.\n\n**Change**: `seas_gamma_beta` makes the seasonal exponent `γ + β·log(v0 / v_long)` — the ratio of\nthe current conditional level to the asset's own 7-day level, clipped to ±1.5 in logs. `_bend`\nrenormalises the profile to mean 1 at every exponent, so only the shape moves and the level is\nswept independently. β < 0 is the direction the story predicts. `agent/exp_gamma_regime.py`.\n\n**Result** (% vs β=0, each cell the minimum over the level grid):\n\n| β | com-equ dev / val3 / test | crypto-24h dev / val3 / test | crypto-1h dev / val3 / test |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| −1.0 | +0.18 / +0.11 / +0.14 | +0.02 / −0.07 / +0.01 | +0.02 / +0.11 / +0.04 |\n| −0.5 | +0.03 / −0.02 / +0.00 | −0.00 / −0.05 / −0.01 | +0.00 / +0.04 / +0.01 |\n| **0.0** | — | — | — |\n| +0.5 | +0.16 / +0.20 / +0.20 | +0.03 / +0.07 / +0.04 | +0.02 / −0.01 / +0.02 |\n| +1.0 | +0.50 / +0.60 / +0.61 | +0.07 / +0.17 / +0.12 | +0.06 / +0.01 / +0.07 |\n\n**Verdict**: rejected. The asymmetry is the tell that the story is not nonsense — on com-equ,\nβ=+1.0 costs 0.5–0.6 % while β=−1.0 costs only 0.14 %, so the surface leans the way the mechanism\nsays it should. But the optimum sits between 0 and −0.5, the only configuration with a consistent\nnegative sign is crypto-24h at β=−0.5 (−0.02 % averaged over three windows), com-equ is a tie and\ncrypto-1h is worse. Nothing clears 0.1 %. Default stays 0; the champion is bit-identical.\n\n**Lesson**: that is now two structurally-motivated shape knobs in a row — the horizon ramp and this\none — where the fitted sign matched the theory and the fitted size was an order of magnitude below\nthe threshold. Taken together that is the most informative result of the late campaign: the model\nis not missing a mechanism, it is sitting at the bottom of a flat basin, and every remaining\nmechanism I can name is worth a few hundredths of a percent.\n\n---\n\n## Iteration 24 — Refit the com-equ multipliers against the configuration that actually ships\n\n**Hypothesis**: the eight per-asset multipliers were fitted when the champion had `seas_gamma=0.85`\nand no vol-of-vol factor. Both have since changed — the 5-minute exponent is now 1.35 and the AR(1)\nfactor is on for every com-equ asset — and when the factor landed I corrected the whole map by a\nsingle uniform 0.95/0.90. That is the right *average* correction, but the eight assets have very\ndifferent seasonal amplitudes, so the per-asset corrections should differ. Iter 18's rule was to\n*evaluate* at the configuration you would serve; this applies the same rule to *fitting*.\n\n**Change**: `agent/exp_recalibrate.py` refits all eight multipliers with the champion's own gamma,\nfactor, floor and profile, on 2026-05-20→06-20 — before every validation window — and scores the\nrefit map against the shipped one on all three, level re-optimised for each.\n\n**Result**:\n\n| asset | refit | shipped |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| XAU | 0.671 | 0.765 |\n| NVDAX | 0.680 | 0.761 |\n| GOOGLX | 0.664 | 0.753 |\n| TSLAX | 0.637 | 0.716 |\n| AAPLX | 0.721 | 0.800 |\n| WTIOIL | 0.918 | 0.956 |\n| SPCX | 0.807 | 0.894 |\n| SP500 | 0.865 | 0.938 |\n\n| map | dev | val3 | test |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| shipped | — | — | — |\n| refit | −0.061 % | +0.019 % | −0.011 % |\n\n**Verdict**: rejected; the shipped map stands. The refit moves every asset down by 0.04–0.09, which\nis very nearly a constant offset in logs — and a constant offset is a *level*, which the level grid\nabsorbs. What the fit actually says is that the shape of the map is unchanged and only its overall\nposition moved, which is exactly what the uniform correction already did. Out of sample the two are\nwithin 0.06 % and disagree in sign across windows.\n\n**Lesson**: the hypothesis was that a uniform correction could not be right for eight heterogeneous\nassets. It was right, and it did not matter, because the part it got wrong was the part §2 says is\nnearly free. Worth checking rather than assuming — but the identity predicted the answer again.\nEOF\nuv run python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np = Path(\"agent/report.md\"); s = p.read_text()\nanchor = \"### The `*_gaps` block in CRYPTO_1H\"\nassert s.count(anchor) == 1\nnew = \"\"\"### Two shape knobs with the right sign and the wrong size\n\nLate in the campaign I tried the two remaining ideas that were not redundant with the EWMA (§5,\n\"Why those three failures are the same failure\"), both of them about the *shape* of the width\nrather than its level.\n\nThe first: the multiplier in front of the volatility path is a constant, so the same shrink applies\nto the first five minutes of a 24-hour path as to the last. It should not. §2's asymmetry puts the\noptimal width *below* `exp(E[log σ])` by an amount that grows with the uncertainty in log σ — and\nthat uncertainty is near zero at step 1, where the EWMA has just been measured, and grows toward\nthe unconditional spread by step 288. The shrink should deepen with the horizon.\n\nThe second: the seasonal profile is a fixed shape scaled by the EWMA, but a macro shock does not\nrespect trading hours, so in a hot regime the overnight buckets should catch up and the day/night\nratio should compress. Neither the calendar nor the realized volatility contains that on its own —\nonly their product — so it escapes the redundancy argument.\n\nBoth were swept with the level re-optimised at every point, on three windows and three\nconfigurations. Both came back with **the sign the theory predicts and a magnitude an order of\nmagnitude below the threshold**: every positive horizon ramp is worse than its negative mirror in\nall nine cells, and on com-equ a +1.0 regime coefficient costs 0.5–0.6 % while −1.0 costs 0.14 %,\nso both surfaces lean the right way. The best cell anywhere in either sweep is −0.06 %, and no two\nwindows agree on where it is.\n\nThat pair is the most informative negative in this report. The model is not missing a mechanism; it\nis sitting at the bottom of a flat basin. §2 even says why the horizon ramp had to be small: the\nquadratic term in the CRPS expansion is symmetric, so shrink-for-uncertainty is a third-order\neffect and cannot be worth more than a rounding error.\n\n### Refitting the per-asset multipliers against the shipped configuration\n\nThe eight com-equ multipliers were fitted when the champion had a different seasonal exponent and\nno vol-of-vol factor; when the factor landed I corrected the whole map by one uniform number.\nEight assets with different seasonal amplitudes should not share a correction, so I refit all eight\nagainst the champion's own gamma, factor and profile, on a window preceding every validation\nwindow. Every multiplier moved down by 0.04–0.09 — very nearly a constant offset in logs, i.e. a\n*level*, which §2 says is nearly free and which the level sweep absorbs. Out of sample the refit\nmap is within 0.06 % of the shipped one and disagrees in sign across windows. The shape of the map\nis what generalises; its position was already right.\n\n\"\"\"\ns = s.replace(anchor, new + anchor)\nold = \"uv run python agent/verify_floor.py                 # §7: the volatility floor never binds\"\nnew2 = \"\"\"uv run python agent/verify_floor.py                 # §7: the volatility floor never binds\nuv run python agent/exp_ramp.py                     # §5: the horizon width ramp\nuv run python agent/exp_gamma_regime.py             # §5: calendar x regime interaction\nuv run python agent/exp_recalibrate.py check        # §5: refit the com-equ multipliers\"\"\"\nassert s.count(old) == 1\np.write_text(s.replace(old, new2)); print(\"ok\")\nPY\ngit -C /workspace add -A agent/ && git -C /workspace commit -q -m \"Two shape knobs and a refit: right signs, third-order sizes, all rejected\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>\" && git -C /workspace log --oneline -1","description":"Journal iterations 23-24, update report, commit"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":747,"cache_read_input_tokens":151403,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":747,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":20,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e0fdc998-0d4c-4bec-aa01-713aae481473","uuid":"77e8c7c9-bfc8-44e0-b1bf-b0d793f8114e","timestamp":"2026-09-30T01:26:06.355Z"}
