{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CfYnmSKiMYd9W2gDsFzm5","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01YUTxPin3rrbj8WQTTcjcu2","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"sed -n '134,156p' agent/report.md","description":"Show the end of report section 4"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":754,"cache_read_input_tokens":72880,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":754,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e0fdc998-0d4c-4bec-aa01-713aae481473","uuid":"21c68ffe-6279-4aab-85a3-ae04c4c08cf5","timestamp":"2026-09-30T01:54:02.647Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01YUTxPin3rrbj8WQTTcjcu2","type":"tool_result","content":"### Fattening the aggregated intervals (−0.10 % on com-equ, three windows)\n\nThe last thing I found, and the only one that came from reading the score's *shape* rather than\nits level. About 39 % of a 24-hour score is charged on intervals longer than one step — 30-min,\n3-hour, 24-hour. (The 1-hour configuration charges a further ~29 % on the cumulative `*_gaps`\nterms, but those turn out to be correctly dispersed already — see §5.) With a σ path\nthat is deterministic given the context, those aggregates are sums of independent innovations,\nso the CLT makes them close to Gaussian; real ones are not, because volatility clusters *inside*\nthe window.\n\nA persistent AR(1) factor in log-volatility fixes that, but only if it is renormalised so every\nstep's variance is exactly unchanged — otherwise it also perturbs the 61 % of the score that was\nalready calibrated, which is why the constant-per-path version of the same idea (`vol_of_vol`,\nthe half-life→∞ limit) had failed earlier. With the constraint it is worth −0.096 %, −0.096 %,\n−0.102 % on three disjoint com-equ windows. On crypto it changes sign between windows and is\nleft off.\n\nIt comes with a level correction that §2 predicts: a fatter mixture at fixed variance is\n*narrower in the bulk*, so the CRPS-optimal width moves up — 0.90 → 0.95, on all three windows.\nShip the shape change without the level change and you give most of it back.\n\n## 5. What didn't work — the more useful half","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e0fdc998-0d4c-4bec-aa01-713aae481473","uuid":"7e63fa4e-6077-4899-996e-5e5a8bc664fc","timestamp":"2026-09-30T01:54:02.671Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"### Fattening the aggregated intervals (−0.10 % on com-equ, three windows)\n\nThe last thing I found, and the only one that came from reading the score's *shape* rather than\nits level. About 39 % of a 24-hour score is charged on intervals longer than one step — 30-min,\n3-hour, 24-hour. (The 1-hour configuration charges a further ~29 % on the cumulative `*_gaps`\nterms, but those turn out to be correctly dispersed already — see §5.) With a σ path\nthat is deterministic given the context, those aggregates are sums of independent innovations,\nso the CLT makes them close to Gaussian; real ones are not, because volatility clusters *inside*\nthe window.\n\nA persistent AR(1) factor in log-volatility fixes that, but only if it is renormalised so every\nstep's variance is exactly unchanged — otherwise it also perturbs the 61 % of the score that was\nalready calibrated, which is why the constant-per-path version of the same idea (`vol_of_vol`,\nthe half-life→∞ limit) had failed earlier. With the constraint it is worth −0.096 %, −0.096 %,\n−0.102 % on three disjoint com-equ windows. On crypto it changes sign between windows and is\nleft off.\n\nIt comes with a level correction that §2 predicts: a fatter mixture at fixed variance is\n*narrower in the bulk*, so the CRPS-optimal width moves up — 0.90 → 0.95, on all three windows.\nShip the shape change without the level change and you give most of it back.\n\n## 5. What didn't work — the more useful half","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CfYnmdnnYx76V3ho2kdAB","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01MLNUQ6nngKmLYa2JYqVoC7","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"uv run python - <<'PY'\np = \"agent/report.md\"\ns = open(p).read()\nanchor = \"\\n## 5. What didn't work — the more useful half\\n\"\nnew = '''\n### Looking back further than the context reaches (−0.04 % on com-equ, three windows)\n\nThe model reverts its conditional volatility toward `v_long`, a slow EWMA of the context. But the\ncontext *is* seven days, so that target is an estimate from one week, and a week that happened to be\nhot leaves a reversion target which is itself an overshoot. Volatility mean-reverts on a monthly\ntimescale too, and nothing inside a seven-day window can see it.\n\nSo I fitted one long-run level per asset and step offline — the RMS of the winsorised log return,\ndeseasonalised by the *same* bent profile the model uses, or the blend would be a units error — and\ncommitted it as an artifact, blending it into the target in logs at weight 0.2. On com-equ that is\nworth −0.168 / −0.026 / −0.039 % on the three windows at the width that ships, with no level\ncorrection of its own. Crypto changes sign between windows at 24 hours and is flat at one hour, and\nis left at 0.\n\nTwo honest caveats. First, the size is *below* the 0.1 % bar everything else here had to clear; it\nis shipped on the consistency of the sign — negative at every blend from 0.1 to 0.3, at four of five\nwidths, in 21 of 24 asset-window cells, and under two vintages of the artifact — rather than on the\nmagnitude. Second, I expected staleness to explain why the first window is four times better than\nthe others, and it does not: refitting the level immediately before each window buys 0.007 pp. The\nfirst window is just a kind one.\n\nThe reason this is worth a paragraph despite being the smallest thing in this section is the\nprinciple underneath it. §5 argues at length that a feature only helps if it is not already flowing\nthrough realized volatility, and I had taken that to mean *forward-looking* information. This\nfeature is entirely backward-looking. What makes it non-redundant is that it looks back further than\nthe served context extends — the interface hands the model seven days, so anything on a longer\ntimescale is outside it by construction and can only get in as an artifact. That is a second source\nof non-redundant information, and unlike the forward-looking one, it needs no data I was not\nalready allowed to have.\n'''\nassert s.count(anchor) == 1\ns = s.replace(anchor, new + anchor)\n\nold = \"uv run python agent/exp_recalibrate.py check        # §5: refit the com-equ multipliers\\n\"\nnew2 = old + \"\"\"uv run python agent/vlong.py                        # §4: build the long-run level artifacts\nuv run python agent/exp_vlong.py                    # §4: sweep the long-run blend weight\n\"\"\"\nassert s.count(old) == 1\ns = s.replace(old, new2)\nopen(p, \"w\").write(s)\nprint(\"ok\")\nPY","description":"Add the long-run level subsection and script entries to the report"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":643,"cache_read_input_tokens":73634,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":643,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e0fdc998-0d4c-4bec-aa01-713aae481473","uuid":"a71bbdd1-57ba-45a7-8087-356c87a9f66d","timestamp":"2026-09-30T01:54:15.000Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MLNUQ6nngKmLYa2JYqVoC7","type":"tool_result","content":"ok","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e0fdc998-0d4c-4bec-aa01-713aae481473","uuid":"c65d4ab6-c9a2-42b7-aee7-30b51f271bbb","timestamp":"2026-09-30T01:54:15.099Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"ok","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"bashEditDiff":{"files":[{"filePath":"/workspace/agent/report.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":152,"oldLines":6,"newStart":152,"newLines":36,"lines":[" *narrower in the bulk*, so the CRPS-optimal width moves up — 0.90 → 0.95, on all three windows."," Ship the shape change without the level change and you give most of it back."," ","+### Looking back further than the context reaches (−0.04 % on com-equ, three windows)","+","+The model reverts its conditional volatility toward `v_long`, a slow EWMA of the context. But the","+context *is* seven days, so that target is an estimate from one week, and a week that happened to be","+hot leaves a reversion target which is itself an overshoot. Volatility mean-reverts on a monthly","+timescale too, and nothing inside a seven-day window can see it.","+","+So I fitted one long-run level per asset and step offline — the RMS of the winsorised log return,","+deseasonalised by the *same* bent profile the model uses, or the blend would be a units error — and","+committed it as an artifact, blending it into the target in logs at weight 0.2. On com-equ that is","+worth −0.168 / −0.026 / −0.039 % on the three windows at the width that ships, with no level","+correction of its own. Crypto changes sign between windows at 24 hours and is flat at one hour, and","+is left at 0.","+","+Two honest caveats. First, the size is *below* the 0.1 % bar everything else here had to clear; it","+is shipped on the consistency of the sign — negative at every blend from 0.1 to 0.3, at four of five","+widths, in 21 of 24 asset-window cells, and under two vintages of the artifact — rather than on the","+magnitude. Second, I expected staleness to explain why the first window is four times better than","+the others, and it does not: refitting the level immediately before each window buys 0.007 pp. The","+first window is just a kind one.","+","+The reason this is worth a paragraph despite being the smallest thing in this section is the","+principle underneath it. §5 argues at length that a feature only helps if it is not already flowing","+through realized volatility, and I had taken that to mean *forward-looking* information. This","+feature is entirely backward-looking. What makes it non-redundant is that it looks back further than","+the served context extends — the interface hands the model seven days, so anything on a longer","+timescale is outside it by construction and can only get in as an artifact. That is a second source","+of non-redundant information, and unlike the forward-looking one, it needs no data I was not","+already allowed to have.","+"," ## 5. What didn't work — the more useful half"," "," ### Median-based seasonality: +10 % on XAU"]},{"oldStart":603,"oldLines":6,"newStart":633,"newLines":8,"lines":[" uv run python agent/exp_ramp.py                     # §5: the horizon width ramp"," uv run python agent/exp_gamma_regime.py             # §5: calendar x regime interaction"," uv run python agent/exp_recalibrate.py check        # §5: refit the com-equ multipliers","+uv run python agent/vlong.py                        # §4: build the long-run level artifacts","+uv run python agent/exp_vlong.py                    # §4: sweep the long-run blend weight"," ```"," "," `agent/harness.py` + `agent/evaluate.py` + `agent/sweep.py` are the fast iteration loop — 545"]}]}],"moreFiles":0,"changedFiles":["/workspace/agent/report.md"]}}}
