is sweep had val3's optimal level pinned at the top of the grid for every hinged","+cell. By the rule written one iteration earlier those cells were not yet comparisons, so I widened","+the grid to 1.15 and re-ran; every argmin is now interior and the numbers are unchanged. The rule","+cost twenty minutes and bought the right to believe the table.","+","+At the shipped level (0.95, 1000 paths, no grid), g_lo = 0.40 gives **−0.250 / −0.076 / −0.059 %**,","+negative in 13 of 15 asset-window cells — only XRP is flat-to-positive on val3 and test. The shipped","+level is already the optimum on two of three windows, so again no second knob.","+","+**Verdict**: shipped, `seas_gamma_lo = {5: 1.35, 1: 0.40}`. The high side keeps 0.85.","+","+**Lesson**: the direction is the same as com-equ's — the quiet end of the calendar is under-served —","+and it is the *opposite* of what the 1-minute damping story predicts on its own. \"1-minute prints","+carry bounce and zero-return minutes, so damp the profile\" was right on average and wrong at the","+quiet end, where the bounce is a larger share of a smaller number and the true width is closer to","+the weekly mean than any power of the profile puts it. Two independent horizons, two asset classes","+and two opposite average exponents all agree that a single power is the wrong shape. That is the","+result worth carrying out of this campaign, more than either number."]}]}],"moreFiles":0,"changedFiles":["/workspace/agent/journal.md"]}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CfYzHSn7kLN7bbQo2c5yv","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1226,"cache_read_input_tokens":159886,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1226,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e0fdc998-0d4c-4bec-aa01-713aae481473","uuid":"8e55a459-d7c2-4d83-8bac-3175e30a7e40","timestamp":"2026-09-30T04:25:08.113Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CfYzHSn7kLN7bbQo2c5yv","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01BzxMeeEvdHtndKhmVX4cKG","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"uv run python - <<'PY'\np=\"agent/report.md\"; s=open(p).read()\nanchor = \"\\n### Looking back further than the context reaches\"\nnew = '''\n### The calendar's effect on width is convex (−0.13 % com-equ, −0.13 % crypto-1h)\n\nThe largest win of the campaign was the time-of-week profile, and the second largest was the\nexponent applied to it. The exponent had been swept four times, always landing near 1.35 at the\n5-minute step with a clean interior optimum. What I had never done was look at the *residual*.\n\n`agent/eda_profile.py` pools every 5-minute step of every simulated path and reports\nmean|realized| / mean|simulated| by decile of the bent profile value — the same statistic as the\ngaps diagnostic, on off-hour cadences for the same reason. For com-equ it comes out U-shaped:\n\n| profile decile | 0.34 | 0.53 | 0.69 | 0.83 | 0.98 | 1.17 | 1.47 | 2.09 |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| ratio (>1 = too narrow) | 1.213 | 1.069 | 1.027 | 0.999 | 0.945 | 1.018 | 1.050 | 1.153 |\n| % of realized steps exactly zero | 16.1 | 7.4 | 4.6 | 3.3 | 2.8 | 2.0 | 1.4 | 1.0 |\n\nRelative to an average bucket the model is too narrow at *both* ends of the calendar at once. A\npower of the profile is linear in log-profile, so no value of the exponent can produce that shape;\nthe sweeps had been finding the best straight line through a curve.\n\nThe bottom row explains the left half. The deadest decile has 16 % of its realized 5-minute\nreturns *exactly zero* and still a 21 % larger mean absolute size than the ensemble. A closed\nsession is not a quiet diffusion — it is a point mass at zero plus occasional gap-sized prints —\nand the CRPS-optimal width for that mixture is wider than the width that matches its bulk. The\nbuckets where the volatility estimator sees the least action are precisely the ones it under-serves.\n\nThe cheapest way to buy convexity is a hinge: one exponent above the weekly mean and another below.\n`sv_model` had carried an unused `seas_gamma_lo` for exactly this shape since an earlier experiment\nand it had never been swept. Swept jointly it gives a broad diagonal ridge, interior on both axes,\nand (1.80, 0.90) is where three disjoint windows agree most evenly: −0.178 / −0.058 / −0.126 %.\n\nThe same thing happens at the 1-minute step, where the shipped exponent is a *damping* (0.85, on\nthe grounds that bid-ask bounce and zero-return minutes are width that does not follow the\ncalendar). That argument is about the average minute and says nothing about the two sides\nseparately. Dropping the quiet-side exponent to 0.40 is worth −0.250 / −0.076 / −0.059 % on\ncrypto-1h, in 13 of 15 asset-window cells. Two horizons, two asset classes, two opposite average\nexponents — and all of them say a single power is the wrong shape and the quiet end of the calendar\nis under-served.\n\nCrypto at the 24-hour horizon is the exception and it is worth reading §5 for why: it looked like a\n−0.05 % win and was a level difference in disguise.\n'''\nassert s.count(anchor)==1; s=s.replace(anchor, new+anchor)\nold = \"uv run python agent/exp_vlong.py                    # §4: sweep the long-run blend weight\\n\"\nnew2 = old + \"\"\"uv run python agent/eda_profile.py                  # §4: dispersion by time-of-week decile\nuv run python agent/exp_gamma_split.py              # §4: the two-sided profile exponent\nuv run python agent/nufit.py                        # §5: per-asset tail indices\nuv run python agent/exp_nu.py                       # §5: sweep the tail-index blend\n\"\"\"\nassert s.count(old)==1; s=s.replace(old,new2)\nopen(p,\"w\").write(s); print(\"ok\")\nPY\ncat /tmp/off_1h.log 2>/dev/null","description":"Add the convexity section to the report"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1226,"cache_read_input_tokens":159886,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1226,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e0fdc998-0d4c-4bec-aa01-713aae481473","uuid":"0ee0a758-8c42-4d99-9c8e-09f130b3d0ae","timestamp":"2026-09-30T04:25:23.350Z"}
