{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01F7ecTapsct5ncPRnZdDW4i","type":"tool_result","content":"def store_root(asset: str) -> Path:\n    \"\"\"Where this asset's partitions live inside the snapshot.\n\n    Resolved by synth-lib rather than hardcoded: the canonical directory is `market_data/prices/`,\n    with a fallback to the legacy `market_data/pyth/` when that is what the snapshot contains. Do\n    Anchored on WORKSPACE, not the cwd — synth-lib's default_store_root() resolves relative to the\n    cwd, which would break the invariant this file relies on everywhere else.\"\"\"\n    return WORKSPACE / \"market_data\" / STORE_SUBDIR / asset / \"1m\"\n\n\ndef check_snapshot_coverage(asset: str, start: datetime, end: datetime) -> None:\n    \"\"\"Fails EARLY and clearly if the requested window falls outside the frozen snapshot.\n\n    Without this guard, synth-lib would try to ingest the missing days (network + write) and\n    the error would surface in an unreadable form.\"\"\"\n    root = store_root(asset)\n    missing = []\n    cursor = start.date()\n    while cursor <= end.date():\n        if not (root / f\"date={cursor.isoformat()}.parquet\").exists():\n            missing.append(cursor.isoformat())\n        cursor += timedelta(days=1)\n    if missing:\n        raise SystemExit(\n            f\"window outside snapshot for {asset}: missing partitions {missing[:5]}\"\n            f\"{' …' if len(missing) > 5 else ''}\\n\"\n            f\"The snapshot is frozen (read-only) and stops at the campaign's data_cutoff. \"\n            f\"Reduce --eval-end / --days to stay within the available coverage ({root}).\"\n        )\n\n\ndef load_context_frame(asset: str, start: datetime, end: datetime) -> pd.DataFrame:\n    \"\"\"Minute OHLCV for a window, tolerating gaps.\n\n    `MinutePriceStore.load_range` raises on a single NaN close. That is right for scoring, and\n    wrong for building the context: the model contract states that `context_prices` MAY CONTAIN\n    NaN (thin markets, feed outages) and that the model must drop rather than interpolate. With\n    the strict loader, `generate()` cannot produce a prediction for ANY com-equ asset, or HYPE,\n    over a multi-day window -- it aborts with \"Local store has N missing minute prices\".\n\n    So the partitions are read directly (the lenient pattern) and reindexed onto a gapless minute\n    grid, which is what makes `[-CONTEXT_MINUTES:]` mean seven *calendar* days rather than the\n    last 10080 rows that happen to exist. NOTHING about scoring changes: `backtest()` below is\n    untouched and still assembles realized paths itself.\n    \"\"\"\n    root = store_root(asset)\n    frames, cursor = [], start.date()\n    while cursor <= end.date():\n        path = root / f\"date={cursor.isoformat()}.parquet\"\n        if path.exists():\n            frames.append(pd.read_parquet(path))","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e0fdc998-0d4c-4bec-aa01-713aae481473","uuid":"d9a48a50-bc7d-4c98-9a69-b562f08ff18d","timestamp":"2026-09-30T05:00:18.506Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"def store_root(asset: str) -> Path:\n    \"\"\"Where this asset's partitions live inside the snapshot.\n\n    Resolved by synth-lib rather than hardcoded: the canonical directory is `market_data/prices/`,\n    with a fallback to the legacy `market_data/pyth/` when that is what the snapshot contains. Do\n    Anchored on WORKSPACE, not the cwd — synth-lib's default_store_root() resolves relative to the\n    cwd, which would break the invariant this file relies on everywhere else.\"\"\"\n    return WORKSPACE / \"market_data\" / STORE_SUBDIR / asset / \"1m\"\n\n\ndef check_snapshot_coverage(asset: str, start: datetime, end: datetime) -> None:\n    \"\"\"Fails EARLY and clearly if the requested window falls outside the frozen snapshot.\n\n    Without this guard, synth-lib would try to ingest the missing days (network + write) and\n    the error would surface in an unreadable form.\"\"\"\n    root = store_root(asset)\n    missing = []\n    cursor = start.date()\n    while cursor <= end.date():\n        if not (root / f\"date={cursor.isoformat()}.parquet\").exists():\n            missing.append(cursor.isoformat())\n        cursor += timedelta(days=1)\n    if missing:\n        raise SystemExit(\n            f\"window outside snapshot for {asset}: missing partitions {missing[:5]}\"\n            f\"{' …' if len(missing) > 5 else ''}\\n\"\n            f\"The snapshot is frozen (read-only) and stops at the campaign's data_cutoff. \"\n            f\"Reduce --eval-end / --days to stay within the available coverage ({root}).\"\n        )\n\n\ndef load_context_frame(asset: str, start: datetime, end: datetime) -> pd.DataFrame:\n    \"\"\"Minute OHLCV for a window, tolerating gaps.\n\n    `MinutePriceStore.load_range` raises on a single NaN close. That is right for scoring, and\n    wrong for building the context: the model contract states that `context_prices` MAY CONTAIN\n    NaN (thin markets, feed outages) and that the model must drop rather than interpolate. With\n    the strict loader, `generate()` cannot produce a prediction for ANY com-equ asset, or HYPE,\n    over a multi-day window -- it aborts with \"Local store has N missing minute prices\".\n\n    So the partitions are read directly (the lenient pattern) and reindexed onto a gapless minute\n    grid, which is what makes `[-CONTEXT_MINUTES:]` mean seven *calendar* days rather than the\n    last 10080 rows that happen to exist. NOTHING about scoring changes: `backtest()` below is\n    untouched and still assembles realized paths itself.\n    \"\"\"\n    root = store_root(asset)\n    frames, cursor = [], start.date()\n    while cursor <= end.date():\n        path = root / f\"date={cursor.isoformat()}.parquet\"\n        if path.exists():\n            frames.append(pd.read_parquet(path))","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
