{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CfZ339gg4UA8MU1xRMTd7","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01XRfYMNtnPZWeGoDvzM33ZS","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"uv run python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np = Path(\"agent/suggestions.md\"); s = p.read_text()\n\nold12 = \"\"\"    dependence on trading intensity, which suggests one profile-indexed function could replace\n    nine hand-fitted numbers and generalise to a new asset with no fitting at all.\n\"\"\"\nnew12 = old12 + \"\"\"\n    **Partly done, Iterations 27–28.** The crudest profile-indexed version of this — one\n    exponent above the weekly mean and another below it — is now shipped, and it was the\n    largest remaining win of the campaign (−0.13 % on com-equ, −0.13 % on crypto-1h). It does\n    not replace the nine constants; it is a second, orthogonal correction. The full version in\n    this item is still open, and the reason to want it is unchanged: nine numbers fitted per\n    asset will not transfer to an asset that joins the competition tomorrow, and a function of\n    the profile value would.\n\"\"\"\nassert s.count(old12) == 1\ns = s.replace(old12, new12)\n\nanchor = \"\\n## What did not work, and the reason they share\\n\"\nitem15 = \"\"\"\n15. **A quadratic bend, and why I am telling you not to bother.** After the two-sided exponent\n    shipped, the com-equ residual was no longer a U but a hump about the hinge point: the model\n    is ~4 % too narrow near the weekly mean and correct at both extremes. The obvious next knob\n    is a quadratic in log-profile instead of two linear pieces. Price it before you fit it. The\n    spread of bucket width errors is `Var(log ratio) ≈ 9e-4` across deciles, and the identity in\n    item 8 turns that straight into a CRPS ceiling of **0.02 %** — below the noise floor of a\n    three-window comparison. The crypto-24h residual, which looks far more inviting (a clean\n    monotone 1.175 → 1.044 gradient, reproduced at two cadences), prices at **0.06 %**, and when\n    I measured it on CRPS directly it came in at **+0.004 %**. Both are closed by arithmetic.\n\"\"\"\nassert s.count(anchor) == 1\ns = s.replace(anchor, item15 + anchor)\n\ns = s.rstrip(\"\\n\") + \"\"\"\n\n19. **Price a residual in CRPS units before fitting it.** A diagnostic that shows structure is\n    not a to-do list; it is a claim that needs a magnitude. Item 8's identity converts a table\n    of per-bucket width ratios into an upper bound on the achievable gain in about a minute of\n    arithmetic — `0.25 · Var(log ratio)` — and that minute decides whether a day of sweeping is\n    worth spending. Two of the three residuals I chased this campaign were worth under 0.1 %\n    before I started, and I only checked first on the third.\n\n20. **Sign consistency across disjoint windows is separate evidence from magnitude, and is the\n    one that survives.** Two changes shipped this campaign on effects near 0.05 % — far too\n    small to call individually — because they were signed the same way in 13 of 15, and in 3 of\n    3, asset-window cells. Two others with larger headline gains were rejected because the sign\n    flipped between windows. Count cells, not basis points.\n\"\"\"\np.write_text(s)\n\nr = Path(\"agent/report.md\"); t = r.read_text()\nprint(t[t.index(\"## 9. Reproducing this\"):][:1800])\nPY","description":"Update suggestions with the closing items"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1867,"cache_read_input_tokens":57085,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1867,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e0fdc998-0d4c-4bec-aa01-713aae481473","uuid":"ce541b64-538e-4f24-bafd-ec56af4960b8","timestamp":"2026-09-30T05:01:30.450Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XRfYMNtnPZWeGoDvzM33ZS","type":"tool_result","content":"## 9. Reproducing this\n\n```bash\nuv run python agent/seasonality.py                  # rebuild the time-of-week artifacts\nuv run python agent/calibrate.py dev                # refit the per-asset multipliers\nuv run agent/predict.py --asset BTC --days 7 --eval-end 2026-09-13 --num-simulations 1000\nbash agent/official_eval.sh    agent /tmp/off.json /tmp/off.log   # the 13 twenty-four-hour runs\nbash agent/official_eval_1h.sh agent /tmp/off.json /tmp/off.log   # the 5 one-hour runs\nuv run python agent/results_table.py off_champ.json off_base.json # renders the tables in §6\nuv run python agent/eda_range.py                    # §5: range estimators, the diagnostic\nuv run python agent/eda_activity.py                 # §5: activity features, the diagnostic\nuv run python agent/exp_activity_crps.py            # §5: activity features, the CRPS test\nuv run python agent/exp_resolution.py               # §5: time-of-week resolution (24h legs)\nuv run python agent/exp_resolution.py 1h            # §5: the same at the 1-minute step\nuv run python agent/exp_scale_1h.py                 # §5: the width level at the 1-minute step\nuv run python agent/exp_leverage.py                 # §5: the leverage effect, both forms\nuv run python agent/exp_vov.py                      # §4: the AR(1) vol-of-vol factor\nuv run python agent/eda_gaps.py                     # §5: per-term dispersion, CRYPTO_1H\nuv run python agent/verify_floor.py                 # §7: the volatility floor never binds\nuv run python agent/exp_ramp.py                     # §5: the horizon width ramp\nuv run python agent/exp_gamma_regime.py             # §5: calendar x regime interaction\nuv run python agent/exp_recalibrate.py check        # §5: refit the com-equ multipliers\nuv run python agent/vlong.py                        # §4: buil","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e0fdc998-0d4c-4bec-aa01-713aae481473","uuid":"360ad025-f90c-407a-8a23-86b147eb48d7","timestamp":"2026-09-30T05:01:30.554Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"## 9. Reproducing this\n\n```bash\nuv run python agent/seasonality.py                  # rebuild the time-of-week artifacts\nuv run python agent/calibrate.py dev                # refit the per-asset multipliers\nuv run agent/predict.py --asset BTC --days 7 --eval-end 2026-09-13 --num-simulations 1000\nbash agent/official_eval.sh    agent /tmp/off.json /tmp/off.log   # the 13 twenty-four-hour runs\nbash agent/official_eval_1h.sh agent /tmp/off.json /tmp/off.log   # the 5 one-hour runs\nuv run python agent/results_table.py off_champ.json off_base.json # renders the tables in §6\nuv run python agent/eda_range.py                    # §5: range estimators, the diagnostic\nuv run python agent/eda_activity.py                 # §5: activity features, the diagnostic\nuv run python agent/exp_activity_crps.py            # §5: activity features, the CRPS test\nuv run python agent/exp_resolution.py               # §5: time-of-week resolution (24h legs)\nuv run python agent/exp_resolution.py 1h            # §5: the same at the 1-minute step\nuv run python agent/exp_scale_1h.py                 # §5: the width level at the 1-minute step\nuv run python agent/exp_leverage.py                 # §5: the leverage effect, both forms\nuv run python agent/exp_vov.py                      # §4: the AR(1) vol-of-vol factor\nuv run python agent/eda_gaps.py                     # §5: per-term dispersion, CRYPTO_1H\nuv run python agent/verify_floor.py                 # §7: the volatility floor never binds\nuv run python agent/exp_ramp.py                     # §5: the horizon width ramp\nuv run python agent/exp_gamma_regime.py             # §5: calendar x regime interaction\nuv run python agent/exp_recalibrate.py check        # §5: refit the com-equ multipliers\nuv run python agent/vlong.py                        # §4: buil","stderr":